// Bun Snapshot v1, https://bun.sh/docs/test/snapshots

exports[`snapshots of the layout IR as text README.md @ 80 1`] = `
"▌ Sample markdown

Test fixtures for rendering, parsing and layout. This is the raw material for
plan item 0.5 (the fixture vault) — not the fixture vault itself, which needs a
.slipbox/ marker and a directory shape these files do not yet have.

Most of these are real documents, copied from the dotfiles repo on 2026-09-25
rather than written to be convenient. That is the point: they contain the
awkward things real notes contain, including markdownlint violations the
originals never fixed.

▌ What each file is for

┌────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────┬─────────────┐
│ File                   │ Exercises                             │ Shape       │
╞════════════════════════╪═══════════════════════════════════════╪═════════════╡
│ wide-tables.md         │ The allocator's hardest case — the    │ 72 lines,   │
│                        │ problem that started the project      │ longest 503 │
├────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────┤
│ overlong-table-rows.md │ Rows at 371 characters, to be wrapped │ 151 lines,  │
│                        │ or refused, never overflowed          │ 34 rows     │
├────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────┤
│ dense-tables.md        │ 232 table rows in one document, most  │ 324 lines   │
│                        │ near the 120 limit. Throughput, not   │             │
│                        │ width                                 │             │
├────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────┤
│ wrap-probe.md          │ A small wide-table case, for fast     │ 16 lines    │
│                        │ iteration                             │             │
├────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────┤
│ frontmatter-tables-    │ YAML frontmatter, 38 table rows, 10   │ 233 lines   │
│ fences.md              │ fenced blocks — the common mixture    │             │
├────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────┤
│ code-heavy.md          │ 22 fenced blocks in several           │ 199 lines   │
│                        │ languages; highlighting and fence     │             │
│                        │ width                                 │             │
├────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────┤
│ large-document.md      │ 1,181 lines, 69 rows, deep nesting.   │ 56 KB       │
│                        │ Scroll, viewport rendering, reparse   │             │
├────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────┤
│ long-form-mixed.md     │ Long prose with embedded code, tables │ 473 lines   │
│                        │ and quotes — the ordinary case at     │             │
│                        │ length                                │             │
├────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────┤
│ links-and-unicode.md   │ Wikilinks and display width.          │ 91 lines    │
│                        │ Synthetic — see below                 │             │
└────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────┴─────────────┘

▌ Provenance

Every file except links-and-unicode.md is a copy. Originals, in
~/Projects/dotfiles:

┌──────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Sample                       │ Copied from                                  │
╞══════════════════════════════╪══════════════════════════════════════════════╡
│ wide-tables.md               │ tmp/markdown-wrap-test.md                    │
│ wrap-probe.md                │ tmp/wrap-probe.md                            │
│ frontmatter-tables-fences.md │ shared/claude/skills/markdown-style/SKILL.md │
│ code-heavy.md                │ shared/claude/skills/nvim-remote/SKILL.md    │
│ overlong-table-rows.md       │ docs/workslate-overview.md                   │
│ large-document.md            │ docs/decisions.md                            │
│ dense-tables.md              │ docs/cheatsheets/nvim.md                     │
│ long-form-mixed.md           │ docs/plans/nvim-agent-integration.md         │
└──────────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────┘

They are snapshots. The originals keep changing; do not expect these to track
them.

▌ The one synthetic file, and why it had to be written

links-and-unicode.md is not a copy. Nothing in the source repository contained a
single wikilink — measured, not assumed — so the feature slipbox exists for had
no test material at all. Nor was there meaningful CJK, emoji or RTL text.

It covers, deliberately: plain and piped wikilinks, an unresolved link, a
heading reference, a block reference, an embed, wikilinks inside a fenced block
and inside inline code that must not resolve, an escaped literal, and the three
ordinary markdown link forms. Then display width — CJK at two columns per
character, emoji, a ZWJ family sequence that is one grapheme across several code
points, combining marks, and an RTL run. Then a table whose rows have equal
character counts and unequal display widths, which is exactly what breaks column
alignment.

It also contains a malformed heading-then-table with no blank line, on purpose.

▌ Still missing

Known gaps, so nobody mistakes this folder for complete coverage. The vault
cases (name collisions, CRLF, a BOM, Latin-1, no trailing newline, a local
image, a .slipbox/ marker, nested directories and links that cross them) are
covered by the test vault, tests/fixtures/vault/, rather than here. Still
missing anywhere:

• A pathological file. No multi-megabyte document, and no .md file that is
  really a log.
• A broken image path. The test vault has a local image, but none that fails to
  load.
• Mermaid and LaTeX. Deferred features, but fixtures cost nothing to add now."
`;

exports[`snapshots of the layout IR as text code-heavy.md @ 80 1`] = `
"── ≡ properties ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 name         nvim-remote
 description  Drive a running nvim instance (in a sibling Herdr pane or
              elsewhere in the current git worktree) via nvim's --server RPC.
              Use when the user explicitly mentions nvim, asks to open/read/edit
              a file in their live editor, or wants to inspect editor state. Do
              not use for general file edits — Edit/Write tools are safer for
              those. Requires an \`nv\` session already running in the current git
              worktree.
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

▌ nvim-remote

Drive a running nvim instance in the current git worktree over its --server
socket. The user launches nvim via nv (a shell function that starts nvim with a
socket keyed off the git worktree root). Any pane in the same worktree —
including this agent — can then talk to that nvim via nvim --server $sock
--remote-*.

▌ When to use

Use only when the user explicitly asks to:

• Open a file in their live editor
• Jump to a line/symbol in what they're editing
• Read the file/buffer they're currently working on
• Save the current buffer
• Run a vim command in their session

Do not use for general file edits. The Edit and Write tools operate on disk
directly, without going through nvim's buffer, and are safer for automation.
This skill is for editor-integrated workflows where the user wants their live
view to update.

▌ Requirement — an nv session must already exist

Compute the socket path from the git worktree root:

                                                                           bash
 root=$(git -C "$PWD" rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null) || root="$PWD"
 key="wt-$(printf '%s' "$root" | shasum -a 256 | cut -c1-12)"
 sock="\${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR:-/tmp}/nvim.\${key}.sock"


Check it exists before doing anything:

                                                                           bash
 [ -S "$sock" ] || { echo "no nv session in this worktree — ask user to run 'nv
↪ <file>' first"; exit 1; }


Never spawn nvim yourself. If no socket exists, tell the user to open nv <file>
in another pane; then re-check.

▌ Learn the current CLI

The installed nvim binary is the authority. Print the remote flags:

                                                                           bash
 nvim --help | grep -E '\\-\\-remote|\\-\\-server' | head -20


Do not rely on memorized syntax — nvim's remote flags occasionally change across
versions.

▌ Open a file

                                                                           bash
 nvim --server "$sock" --remote-tab file.rs


At a specific line:

                                                                           bash
 nvim --server "$sock" --remote-tab +42 file.rs


At line + column:

                                                                           bash
 nvim --server "$sock" --remote-tab file.rs
 nvim --server "$sock" --remote-send ':call cursor(42, 10)<CR>'


Prefer --remote-tab over --remote — the latter replaces whatever's in the
current window, which usually surprises the user.

▌ Read editor state

Current file path:

                                                                           bash
 nvim --server "$sock" --remote-expr 'expand("%:p")'


Line under cursor:

                                                                           bash
 nvim --server "$sock" --remote-expr 'getline(".")'


Whole current buffer:

                                                                           bash
 nvim --server "$sock" --remote-expr 'join(getline(1,"$"),"\\n")'


Cursor position [bufnum, lnum, col, off]:

                                                                           bash
 nvim --server "$sock" --remote-expr 'getcurpos()'


List of open (listed) buffers:

                                                                           bash
 nvim --server "$sock" --remote-expr 'map(getbufinfo({"buflisted":1}), "v:val.n
↪ame")'


Currently visible windows and their filenames:

                                                                           bash
 nvim --server "$sock" --remote-expr 'map(getwininfo(), "bufname(v:val.bufnr)")
↪'


▌ Save

Current buffer:

                                                                           bash
 nvim --server "$sock" --remote-send ':w<CR>'


All modified buffers:

                                                                           bash
 nvim --server "$sock" --remote-send ':wall<CR>'


Only tell the user when you save on their behalf — silent saves are surprising.

▌ Run vim commands

For commands whose output you need to capture, wrap in execute():

                                                                           bash
 nvim --server "$sock" --remote-expr 'execute(":messages")'
 nvim --server "$sock" --remote-expr 'execute(":ls")'


For side-effect commands, --remote-send is fine:

                                                                           bash
 nvim --server "$sock" --remote-send ':nohlsearch<CR>'


▌ Run Lua

Value-returning:

                                                                           bash
 nvim --server "$sock" --remote-expr 'luaeval("vim.fn.expand(\\"%:p\\")")'


Side-effect (returns nil, no need to capture):

                                                                           bash
 nvim --server "$sock" --remote-expr 'luaeval("vim.diagnostic.setqflist({open=f
↪alse})")'


Multi-line Lua — write to a temp file and luafile:

                                                                           bash
 cat >/tmp/nvim-remote.lua <<'LUA'
 local diags = vim.diagnostic.get(0)
 for _, d in ipairs(diags) do print(d.lnum + 1, d.message) end
 LUA
 nvim --server "$sock" --remote-send ':luafile /tmp/nvim-remote.lua<CR>'


▌ Safety rules

• Never quit the user's session. No :q, :qa, :qa! via --remote-send. If you must
  remove a buffer you opened, use :bd <bufnum> targeting a specific buffer,
  never the current one.

• Never save without telling the user. :w writes to disk; the user should know
  when their file has been modified.

• Escape insert mode first. --remote-send runs verbatim. If the user's cursor is
  in insert mode, your keystrokes insert literal characters. Prefix commands
  with <C-\\><C-N> to force normal mode:

                                                                           bash
   nvim --server "$sock" --remote-send '<C-\\><C-N>:e file.md<CR>'


• Don't steal focus. --remote-tab opens a new tab but doesn't switch tabs on its
  own. Adding --remote-tab-wait blocks until the tab closes — use it only when
  you want to know when the user is done.

• Escape shell metacharacters. Single-quote the whole --remote-expr /
  --remote-send argument. Avoid embedded single quotes (use '' concatenation or
  a heredoc-based approach if you need one).

▌ Multiple nvim / multiple Claude / edge cases

• Multiple Claude sessions, one nvim: fine — both connect to the same socket.
  Commands serialize.
• Multiple nv sessions in the same worktree: the first one owns the socket.
  Subsequent nv invocations detect this and start without --listen — they're
  invisible to this skill. The user knows only their first nv is reachable.
• User closed nvim: the socket file is cleaned up on graceful exit. Re-check [
  -S "$sock" ] before every call.
• Discovering sessions in other worktrees (unusual):

                                                                           bash
   ls "\${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR:-/tmp}"/nvim.wt-*.sock 2>/dev/null
   # for each, query cwd:
   nvim --server "$s" --remote-expr 'getcwd()'


▌ Verify a connection

Fast liveness check:

                                                                           bash
 nvim --server "$sock" --remote-expr 'v:servername'


Should print the socket path back. If it doesn't or errors, the socket is stale
or nvim isn't listening — tell the user, don't retry silently."
`;

exports[`snapshots of the layout IR as text dense-tables.md @ 80 1`] = `
"Leader = Space · Pattern = <count><verb><motion> (e.g. 3dw = delete 3 words) ·
Space + wait → which-key popup

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

▌ Modes

┌─────────────┬─────────┬─────────────┐
│ Key         │ Mode    │ Use         │
╞═════════════╪═════════╪═════════════╡
│ Esc         │ Normal  │ navigation  │
│ i a o I A O │ Insert  │ typing      │
│ v V Ctrl+v  │ Visual  │ selection   │
│ :           │ Command │ run command │
│ / ?         │ Search  │ find        │
└─────────────┴─────────┴─────────────┘

▌ Insert mode — pick the right entry

┌───────────┬───────────────────────┐
│ Key       │ Where                 │
╞═══════════╪═══════════════════════╡
│ i         │ before cursor         │
│ a         │ after cursor (append) │
│ I         │ start of line         │
│ A         │ end of line           │
│ o         │ new line below        │
│ O         │ new line above        │
│ s         │ delete char + insert  │
│ c<motion> │ change (e.g. cw)      │
└───────────┴───────────────────────┘

▌ Insert-mode shortcuts (while typing)

┌─────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Key         │ Action                                   │
╞═════════════╪══════════════════════════════════════════╡
│ <C-w> <C-u> │ delete prev word / to line start         │
│ <C-r><reg>  │ paste register (e.g. <C-r>+ = clipboard) │
│ <C-o> <cmd> │ run ONE normal command, back to insert   │
└─────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────┘

▌ Movement

┌─────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Key                 │ Action                                              │
╞═════════════════════╪═════════════════════════════════════════════════════╡
│ h j k l             │ left / down / up / right                            │
│ w b e               │ next word / prev word / end of word                 │
│ W B E               │ same but WORD-wise (whitespace-separated)           │
│ 0 ^ $ g_            │ line start / first non-blank / end / last non-blank │
│ gg G <N>G           │ top / bottom / line N                               │
│ { }                 │ previous / next paragraph (blank line)              │
│ ( )                 │ previous / next sentence                            │
│ Ctrl+d Ctrl+u       │ half page down / up                                 │
│ Ctrl+f Ctrl+b       │ full page forward / back                            │
│ Ctrl+e Ctrl+y       │ scroll one line down / up (cursor stays)            │
│ zz zt zb            │ center / top / bottom current line                  │
│ f<c> F<c> t<c> T<c> │ jump to / up-to char (;,, repeat)                   │
│ %                   │ jump to matching bracket                            │
│ * #                 │ search word under cursor f/b                        │
└─────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

▌ Treesitter motions (nvim-treesitter-textobjects)

┌─────────┬─────────────────────────────────┐
│ Key     │ Action                          │
╞═════════╪═════════════════════════════════╡
│ ]m [m   │ next / previous function start  │
│ ]] [[   │ next / previous class / section │
│ vaf vif │ select around / inside function │
│ vac vic │ select around / inside class    │
└─────────┴─────────────────────────────────┘

▌ Editing

┌──────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Key          │ Action                              │
╞══════════════╪═════════════════════════════════════╡
│ x            │ delete char                         │
│ dw dd D      │ delete word / line / to end-of-line │
│ cw cc S      │ change word / line / line           │
│ r<c> R       │ replace one char / replace mode     │
│ u Ctrl+r     │ undo / redo                         │
│ .            │ repeat last change (powerful)       │
│ yw yy        │ yank word / line                    │
│ p P          │ paste after / before                │
│ >> <<        │ indent / unindent                   │
│ J            │ join line below                     │
│ ~            │ toggle case                         │
│ <count><cmd> │ e.g. 5dd delete 5 lines             │
└──────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────┘

▌ Visual + Text Objects

┌────────┬─────────────────┐
│ Key    │ Selection       │
╞════════╪═════════════════╡
│ v      │ char-wise       │
│ V      │ line-wise       │
│ Ctrl+v │ block / column  │
│ viw    │ inner word      │
│ vip    │ inner paragraph │
└────────┴─────────────────┘

Text objects — combine with i (inner) or a (around):

┌─────────┬─────────────────────────────┐
│ Object  │ What it grabs               │
╞═════════╪═════════════════════════════╡
│ w s p   │ word / sentence / paragraph │
│ " ' \`   │ quoted text                 │
│ ( [ { < │ bracketed text              │
│ t       │ HTML/XML tag                │
└─────────┴─────────────────────────────┘

Examples: ci" change inside quotes · da( delete around parens · vit select inner
tag · yi{ yank inside braces

▌ Multi-cursor (vim-visual-multi)

┌────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Key        │ Action                                  │
╞════════════╪═════════════════════════════════════════╡
│ Ctrl+n     │ start: select word + add cursor at next │
│ n N        │ skip / back                             │
│ Ctrl+a     │ add cursor at every match in selection  │
│ Ctrl+Up/Dn │ add cursor on line above / below        │
│ q          │ drop current cursor                     │
│ Esc        │ exit multi-cursor                       │
└────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────┘

▌ Surround (mini.surround — bundled)

┌───────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Key           │ Action                               │
╞═══════════════╪══════════════════════════════════════╡
│ sa<motion><c> │ add — saiw" → "word" · visual: sa<c> │
│ sd<c>         │ delete — sd" removes surrounding "   │
│ sr<old><new>  │ replace — sr"' → " becomes '         │
└───────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────┘

▌ Search & Replace

┌──────────────────┬─────────────────────────┐
│ Key              │ Action                  │
╞══════════════════╪═════════════════════════╡
│ /pat ?pat        │ search forward / back   │
│ n N              │ next / previous match   │
│ :noh             │ clear highlight         │
│ :s/old/new/g     │ replace in current line │
│ :%s/old/new/g    │ replace in whole file   │
│ :%s/old/new/gc   │ replace with confirm    │
│ :5,10s/old/new/g │ replace in line range   │
└──────────────────┴─────────────────────────┘

▌ Completion popup (blink.cmp)

┌───────────────┬───────────────────────┐
│ Key           │ Action                │
╞═══════════════╪═══════════════════════╡
│ <C-Space>     │ trigger completion    │
│ <Tab> <S-Tab> │ next / previous item  │
│ <CR> / <C-y>  │ confirm · <C-e> abort │
└───────────────┴───────────────────────┘

▌ Comments (mini.comment — bundled)

┌─────────────┬────────────────────────────────┐
│ Key         │ Action                         │
╞═════════════╪════════════════════════════════╡
│ gcc         │ toggle current line            │
│ gc<motion>  │ toggle range (e.g. gcap, gc3j) │
│ gc (visual) │ toggle selection               │
└─────────────┴────────────────────────────────┘

▌ Folding (collapse code regions)

┌───────┬─────────────────────────────────┐
│ Key   │ Action                          │
╞═══════╪═════════════════════════════════╡
│ za    │ toggle fold at cursor           │
│ zo zc │ open / close fold               │
│ zR zM │ open all / close all folds      │
│ zr zm │ open / close one level globally │
│ zj zk │ jump to next / previous fold    │
└───────┴─────────────────────────────────┘

▌ Markdown helpers

┌───────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Key / command     │ Action                                         │
╞═══════════════════╪════════════════════════════════════════════════╡
│ :RenderMarkdown   │ toggle pretty render (force: enable / disable) │
│ gqap · gqq        │ reflow paragraph / single line to textwidth    │
│ saiw* · saiw\`     │ wrap as *italic* (×2 = **bold**) · \`code\`      │
│ saiw[ then add () │ wrap as [word], then type (url)                │
│ gx                │ open URL / file path under cursor              │
└───────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

▌ Files / Buffers

┌───────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Key               │ Action                                      │
╞═══════════════════╪═════════════════════════════════════════════╡
│ :w :w name :wq ZZ │ save / save-as / save+quit                  │
│ :q :q! :wqa :qa!  │ quit / force / all / force-all              │
│ :e file :e!       │ open / reload from disk                     │
│ :ls               │ list open buffers                           │
│ :b <N> :b <name>  │ switch to buffer                            │
│ :bn :bp :bd       │ next / prev / delete buffer                 │
│ <C-^> (or <C-6>)  │ toggle alternate buffer (most recent other) │
└───────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────┘

▌ Windows / Splits

┌──────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Key                          │ Action                                        │
╞══════════════════════════════╪═══════════════════════════════════════════════╡
│ :sp / Ctrl+w s               │ horizontal split (above/below)                │
├──────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ :vs / Ctrl+w v               │ vertical split (side by side)                 │
├──────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Ctrl+w h/j/k/l · <C-h/j/k/l> │ move focus (vanilla · kickstart shortcut, no  │
│                              │ prefix)                                       │
├──────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Ctrl+w c / Ctrl+w o          │ close current / keep only this                │
├──────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Ctrl+w =                     │ equalize all window sizes                     │
├──────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Ctrl+w > < / + -             │ wider/narrower · taller/shorter               │
└──────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────┘

▌ Tabs (page-level, heavier than splits)

┌─────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────┐
│ Key                     │ Action                     │
╞═════════════════════════╪════════════════════════════╡
│ :tabnew <f> :tabc :tabo │ new / close / close others │
│ gt gT                   │ next / previous tab        │
└─────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────┘

▌ Marks

┌───────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Key               │ Action                                                   │
╞═══════════════════╪══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╡
│ ma · mA–mZ        │ local mark · global (across files, persists across       │
│                   │ sessions)                                                │
├───────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ 'a \`a             │ jump to line / exact position                            │
├───────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ '' \`. \`^ · :marks │ last jump · edit · insert exit · list all                │
└───────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

▌ Registers

┌─────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Key         │ Action                                                   │
╞═════════════╪══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╡
│ :reg        │ list contents of all registers                           │
│ "+y "+p     │ yank / paste system clipboard                            │
│ "_d         │ delete to black hole (preserves yank)                    │
│ "0p · "1–"9 │ paste last yank (survives d/c) · numbered delete history │
└─────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

▌ Jumps & change history

┌──────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────┐
│ Key              │ Action                           │
╞══════════════════╪══════════════════════════════════╡
│ Ctrl+o Ctrl+i    │ jump back / forward (file-aware) │
│ g; g,            │ older / newer edit position      │
│ (backtick×2)     │ back to pos before last jump     │
└──────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────┘

▌ Neo-tree (file tree — open with <Space>e)

┌───────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Key               │ Action                                    │
╞═══════════════════╪═══════════════════════════════════════════╡
│ ?                 │ help overlay inside tree                  │
│ <CR> / o          │ open file / expand folder                 │
│ <C-x> <C-v> <C-t> │ open in hsplit / vsplit / tab             │
│ s S               │ open in vsplit / hsplit (Neo-tree native) │
│ z                 │ collapse all folders in tree              │
│ a                 │ add file (end name with / for dir)        │
│ A                 │ add directory                             │
│ d r               │ delete / rename                           │
│ c m               │ copy / move (then navigate + paste)       │
│ y x p             │ yank / cut / paste path-clipboard         │
│ H R               │ toggle hidden · refresh tree              │
│ q                 │ close tree window                         │
└───────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────┘

▌ Telescope (in any picker)

┌─────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────┐
│ Key                         │ Action                          │
╞═════════════════════════════╪═════════════════════════════════╡
│ <C-n> <C-p> / ↓ ↑           │ next / previous result          │
│ <CR>                        │ open                            │
│ <C-x> <C-v> <C-t>           │ open in hsplit / vsplit / tab   │
│ <Tab>                       │ multi-select (toggle this item) │
│ <C-u> <C-d>                 │ scroll preview up / down        │
│ <C-w>                       │ delete previous word in prompt  │
│ <C-r>" (insert)             │ paste register " into prompt    │
│ <C-/> insert · <Esc> normal │ show keymaps · close            │
└─────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────┘

▌ kickstart (leader = Space)

┌─────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────┐
│ Key                 │ Action                           │
╞═════════════════════╪══════════════════════════════════╡
│ <Space> + wait      │ which-key popup (discover keys)  │
│ <Space>sf           │ search files (fuzzy)             │
│ <Space>sg           │ grep in project (live)           │
│ <Space>s/           │ live grep in open buffers        │
│ <Space>/            │ fuzzy search in current buffer   │
│ <Space>s.           │ recent files                     │
│ <Space><Space>      │ buffer list (Telescope picker)   │
│ <Space>sw           │ search word under cursor         │
│ <Space>sk <Space>sh │ search keymaps / help tags       │
│ <Space>ds           │ document symbols                 │
│ <Space>ws           │ workspace symbols                │
│ <Space>ss           │ pick from all Telescope builtins │
│ <Space>D            │ go to type definition (LSP)      │
│ <Space>e            │ toggle file tree (Neo-tree)      │
│ <Space>th           │ toggle inlay hints               │
└─────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────┘

▌ LSP

┌────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Key            │ Action                                   │
╞════════════════╪══════════════════════════════════════════╡
│ K              │ hover docs                               │
│ <C-k> (insert) │ signature help for current function call │
│ gd gD          │ go to definition / declaration           │
│ gI             │ go to implementation                     │
│ gr             │ find references                          │
│ <Space>rn      │ rename symbol                            │
│ <Space>ca      │ code actions                             │
│ ]d [d          │ next / previous diagnostic               │
│ <C-w>d         │ open diagnostic float for current line   │
└────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────┘

▌ Gitsigns

┌───────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────┐
│ Key                   │ Action                           │
╞═══════════════════════╪══════════════════════════════════╡
│ ]c [c                 │ next / previous git hunk         │
│ <Space>hp             │ preview hunk diff                │
│ <Space>hs · <Space>hr │ stage / reset hunk               │
│ <Space>hb             │ blame line (toggle inline blame) │
│ <Space>hd             │ diff buffer against index        │
└───────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────┘

▌ Ex commands you'll actually use

┌─────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Command                 │ Action                                   │
╞═════════════════════════╪══════════════════════════════════════════╡
│ :g/pat/d                │ delete every line matching pat           │
│ :v/pat/d                │ delete every line NOT matching           │
│ :sort · :sort u         │ sort lines / sort+unique                 │
│ :set list! · :set wrap! │ toggle whitespace / line wrap            │
│ :Inspect · :LspInfo     │ treesitter info · LSP servers for buffer │
│ :edit %:h · :cd %:h     │ open / cd to dir of current file         │
│ :r file · :r !cmd       │ insert file content / shell output       │
│ :earlier 5m · :later 1m │ time-travel undo by clock                │
└─────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────┘

▌ Survival

┌───────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Key                   │ Action                                             │
╞═══════════════════════╪════════════════════════════════════════════════════╡
│ :e!                   │ reload buffer from disk (after swap warning, etc.) │
│ :term · <C-\\><C-n>    │ open terminal · escape terminal mode               │
│ :help <topic>         │ help on anything (:help alone = main menu)         │
│ :checkhealth · :Mason │ nvim diagnostics · install LSPs / formatters       │
│ g- · g+               │ older / newer text state (undo by time, branches)  │
│ :undolist             │ show full undo tree branches                       │
│ Ctrl+g                │ show file / cursor position                        │
└───────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘"
`;

exports[`snapshots of the layout IR as text frontmatter-tables-fences.md @ 80 1`] = `
"── ≡ properties ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 name         markdown-style
 description  Write markdown files that pass this project's markdownlint-cli2
              config on first save (no fix-up loop). Activate whenever creating
              or editing .md files. The linter is strict on line length (120
              chars, applied to tables and code blocks too) and on
              link/heading/list structure. This skill teaches the enforced rules
              and the workarounds — especially for tables that would exceed 120
              chars.
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

▌ markdown-style

Produces markdown that passes the project's ~/.markdownlint-cli2.jsonc config on
the first save. The linter runs in editors (via nvim-lint) and in CI, so files
that don't comply generate noise until fixed.

▌ The one rule that surprises most: 120 chars, EVERYWHERE

Line length is capped at 120 characters for paragraphs, code blocks, tables, AND
headings. No exceptions. Tables and code that would exceed 120 must be
restructured, not left long.

▌ Long lines in paragraphs

Wrap at ~120 chars, ideally at a punctuation boundary:

                                                                             md
 The system distinguishes idle from done based on whether the result has been
 seen — see the state semantics section for the exact transitions.


▌ Long lines in code blocks

Wrap in the source language's natural way. Don't just let them flow long.

                                                                           bash
 # ❌ 140 chars, will lint-error
 curl -X POST https://api.example.com/v1/resources -H "Authorization: Bearer $T
↪OKEN" -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d @body.json

 # ✓ split across lines with backslash-continuation
 curl -X POST https://api.example.com/v1/resources \\
   -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \\
   -H "Content-Type: application/json" \\
   -d @body.json


▌ Long lines in tables — the important case

A pipe-table row is one source line. GFM has no continuation syntax, so a long
row cannot be broken — it can only be made shorter.

Budget the width before writing the table, not after. Borders and padding cost
3N + 1 characters for N columns, leaving this much for content:

┌─────────┬──────────┬────────────────┬────────────┐
│ Columns │ Overhead │ Content budget │ Per column │
╞═════════╪══════════╪════════════════╪════════════╡
│ 2       │ 7        │ 113            │ ~56        │
│ 3       │ 10       │ 110            │ ~36        │
│ 4       │ 13       │ 107            │ ~26        │
│ 5       │ 16       │ 104            │ ~20        │
│ 6       │ 19       │ 101            │ ~16        │
└─────────┴──────────┴────────────────┴────────────┘

Budget the widest cell in each column, not the average — prettier pads every
cell in a column out to its widest member, so the widest one sets the final row
width.

Read that table as a design constraint: at 4+ columns you have room for a
phrase, not a sentence. If cells need sentences, the table gets 2 columns or it
stops being a table. Choosing the column count is choosing how much can go in a
cell.

That also rules out one technique up front:

<br> does NOT shorten a row. It is a rendering control that adds four characters
to the source. Use it to shape how a cell displays, never to get under 120.

Three techniques that do work:

Terse fragments instead of prose — the real lever. Rewrite cell content as
clipped phrases; <br> then only controls where they break:

                                                                             md
 | Command | Purpose |
 | --- | --- |
 | \`pane split\` | new pane<br>direction + ratio<br>honors \`--no-focus\` |


Transpose into sections — the only fix when the content genuinely is long prose.
Each row becomes a section, and the text wraps like any paragraph:

                                                                             md
 ### \`pane split\`

 Creates a new pane. Direction and ratio are configurable, and it honors \`--no-
↪focus\` so the
 caller keeps focus where it was.


Restructure into multiple sub-tables — when a table has 3+ wide cells:

                                                                             md
 ### Basics
 | Field | Value |
 | --- | --- |
 | id | opaque string |
 | status | one of idle / working / done |

 ### Detail
 | Field | Value |
 | --- | --- |
 | description | long-form explanation of what the field means and when it appe
↪ars |


If a table needs <br> chains to survive, the content isn't tabular — transpose
it. Tables are for values you scan across; prose belongs in paragraphs.

Why this rule earns its keep, beyond silencing the linter: render-markdown wraps
any table row wider than the window into virtual lines that replace the source.
The row under the cursor then reverts to raw text, because virtual lines have no
cell for a cursor to sit in — which makes wide tables genuinely painful to edit.
A table that respects 120 never wraps and never does this.

Watch for prettier undoing this. conform runs prettier on markdown, and prettier
pads table cells to align the pipes. That padding counts toward the 120 limit,
so a table you wrote compact can come back over the line after a format-on-save.
Check the width after saving, not before.

▌ Long lines in headings

If a heading is longer than 120 chars, it's a paragraph pretending to be a
heading. Rewrite it as a shorter heading plus a leading paragraph.

▌ Enforced rules — write to these

▌ Headings

• ATX style: ## Foo, never Setext (Foo\\n===)
• Never skip levels: ## → ### → ####, not ## → ####
• One # H1 per file (but the file doesn't need to start with one)
• Blank line before AND after every heading

▌ Lists

• Blank line before AND after every list
• Consistent bullet within a doc — use - throughout, OR * throughout, don't mix
• Ordered lists: use 1. 2. 3. OR all 1. (both accepted; don't mix in one list)
• Consistent indent width within a list

▌ Code blocks

• Always specify a language on fences: \`\`\`bash, \`\`\`json, \`\`\`python, \`\`\`text if
  none
• Blank line before AND after every fence
• Fenced style (\`\`\`) — not indented-4-spaces code

▌ Tables

• Blank line before AND after every table
• Every row has the same column count (missing cells → add empty ones)
• Cell content over ~90 chars → shorten it or transpose the table (see above);
  <br> won't help

▌ Links

• No bare URLs. Always wrap: [https://example.com](https://example.com) even
  when the text IS the URL. <https://example.com> (autolink) is also acceptable.
• No spaces inside link text: [foo](url), not [ foo ](url)
• No empty links: [text]() is invalid
• Valid link fragments: [section](#the-section) — anchor must exist in the file

▌ Images

• Every image needs alt text: ![Description here](url) — never ![](url)

▌ Emphasis

• No spaces inside markers: **bold**, *italic*, \`code\` — never ** bold **, *
  italic *, \` code \`

▌ File hygiene

• End every file with exactly one \\n
• Indent with spaces, no hard tabs
• No trailing whitespace on lines — unless it's exactly 2 spaces (intentional
  <br>)
• At most 2 consecutive blank lines

▌ Rules the project explicitly disables — don't worry about these

These are turned off in ~/.markdownlint-cli2.jsonc for stylistic freedom:

┌───────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┬────┐
│ Rule  │ What it would complain about                              │    │
╞═══════╪═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╪════╡
│ MD004 │ - vs * for unordered list bullets                         │    │
│ MD007 │ Unordered list indent depth                               │    │
│ MD014 │ $ shown before shell commands (or not)                    │    │
│ MD026 │ Trailing punctuation in a heading (., !, ?)               │    │
│ MD028 │ Blank line inside a blockquote                            │    │
│ MD033 │ Inline HTML (<details>, <br>, <sub>, etc. — OK to use)    │    │
│ MD035 │ Horizontal rule style (--- vs ***)                        │    │
│ MD036 │ Bold used where a heading might fit better                │    │
│ MD041 │ File must start with an H1                                │    │
│ MD043 │ Required heading structure (template-based projects only) │    │
│ MD044 │ Proper-name capitalization                                │    │
│ MD046 │ Code block style (fenced vs indented)                     │    │
│ MD048 │ Code fence character (\`\`\` vs ~~~)                         │    │
│ MD049 │ Emphasis marker (* vs _)                                  │    │
│ MD050 │ Strong marker (** vs __)                                  │    │
│ MD054 │ Link/image style                                          │    │
│ MD055 │ Table pipe style (leading/trailing \`                      │ \`) │
│ MD059 │ Whether link text is "descriptive" enough                 │    │
│ MD060 │ Table column style                                        │    │
└───────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┴────┘

Use whichever variant reads best.

▌ Verify before finishing

For any non-trivial markdown file, run:

                                                                           bash
 markdownlint-cli2 <file>


Fix issues before declaring done. Common exit paths:

• 0 exit + no output → file is clean, ship it
• 1 exit + MD013/line-length errors on tables → shorten the cells or transpose,
  per above
• 1 exit + MD040/fenced-code-language → add \`\`\`language to bare fences
• 1 exit + MD034/no-bare-urls → wrap the URLs in [](url) or <> autolinks

▌ Common gotchas — quick reference

1. Long table rows — the #1 gotcha; a row is one line and can only be shortened,
   never broken
2. Bare URL in prose — always wrap it
3. Code block right after a paragraph — need a blank line in between
4. Table right after prose — same, need a blank line
5. Heading followed immediately by another heading — put a blank line between
6. Missing language on \`\`\` fences — always add one, use text for plain output
7. File ends without a newline — always terminate with \\n
8. Skipping heading levels — from ## jump only to ###, never ####

▌ When NOT to use this skill

• Reading markdown (only creating/editing)
• Markdown files outside the project where linting doesn't apply
• Rendered content on GitHub (renderer is more forgiving than the linter — but
  still aim for lint-clean, since editor + CI both check)"
`;

exports[`snapshots of the layout IR as text large-document.md @ 80 1`] = `
"▌ Architecture Decisions

ADR-style log. Add new entries when revisiting choices.

▌ D1: Single repo, top-level mac/vm split (2026-04)

Decision: Single repo, mac/ and vm/ at top level. Why: Single source of truth,
single git history, easy cross-references. The "VMs pull Mac configs they don't
need" downside is negligible.

▌ D2: Stow over chezmoi or hand-rolled bash (2026-04)

Decision: Stow + thin bash orchestration. Why: Stow's package model maps cleanly
to base+overlay strategy. No templating to learn. 30-year-old tool. chezmoi's
templating isn't needed for one Mac + similar VMs.

▌ D3: Layered VM config — base + scope overlay (2026-04)

Decision: Each VM applies vm/base/ first, then vm/scopes/<scope>/. Why: Stow
handles overlay naturally. Editing base updates every VM; editing scope only
changes that scope. Zero duplication.

▌ D4: Use include directives for shared-but-divergent configs (2026-04)

Decision: Where a tool supports include directives, use them. Why: Avoids
duplicating large files for a few diverging lines. Patterns: [includeIf] in git,
Include/Match in SSH, sourced env files in shell.

▌ D5: Curated Brewfile, never regenerated (2026-04)

Decision: Maintain mac/Brewfile by hand. Never brew bundle dump. Why: Brewfile's
purpose is "what does this setup need?" not "what's on this machine right now?"

Adopting hand-installed apps: brew install --cask --adopt <name> can register an
existing /Applications/<App>.app with brew, but it's unreliable for some casks
(Google Chrome leaves the Caskroom slot in a state that breaks the next
install). Safer recipe: quit the app, move it out of /Applications/ (or just
delete it — user data lives under ~/Library/Application Support/<vendor>/, not
in the .app), then run a plain brew install --cask <name>. Reserve --adopt for
casks you've verified handle it cleanly.

▌ D6: Per-environment lifecycle scripts (2026-04)

Decision: Each environment (mac/, vm/) has separate scripts for install (fresh
machine bootstrap), switchover (first-time Stow on existing Mac with live
configs), apply (reload tools after editing), and restow (after adding/removing
files). Why: Different operations have different lifecycles and risk profiles.
Conflating them into a single "do everything" script encourages reckless re-runs
and obscures what's safe vs dangerous.

▌ D7: AeroSpace over yabai (earlier) — superseded by D23

▎ Superseded by D23 (2026-05). AeroSpace was replaced with macOS Spaces +
▎ Rectangle. The original choice over yabai stands as rationale for why not
▎ yabai; the rest is historical.

Decision: AeroSpace. Why: No SIP-disable required. TOML config readable.
Letter-named workspaces match scope mental model.

▌ D8: SketchyBar at bottom, transparent floating islands (earlier)

Decision: Bottom, transparent bar, items grouped into rounded islands. Why:
Pe8er-style aesthetic. Each island is a logical group.

▌ D9: JankyBorders for focused-window indication (earlier)

Decision: JankyBorders with workspace-keyed colors. Why: Native to
SketchyBar/AeroSpace ecosystem. Tracks focus across monitors. Layers with
SketchyBar pill colors.

▌ D10: Native Swift watchers for live indicators (2026-04)

Decision: SketchyBar items that need event-driven, system-wide signals (camera
in use, OBS recording) are backed by small Swift binaries under mac/bin/, run as
user LaunchAgents. Each watcher writes a state file under
~/.cache/sketchybar/<name>-state and triggers a custom sketchybar event on
transitions. The matching plugin script just renders the file.

Why: Polling approaches were either unreliable (pgrep VDCAssistant returns true
long after streaming ends; modern macOS redacts unified-log camera events) or
wasteful (re-running shell scripts every few seconds for state that rarely
changes). Native APIs give an exact signal at ~0% idle CPU:

• kCMIODevicePropertyDeviceIsRunningSomewhere (CoreMediaIO) for any app's camera
  use.
• OBS WebSocket v5 RecordStateChanged event for OBS-specific state.

How to apply: When adding a new live indicator, prefer this pattern over
update_freq polling whenever a push API exists. Source goes in
mac/bin/<name>.swift, build line in mac/bin/build.sh, registration in
mac/apply.sh via install_agent <name>. The compiled binary is gitignored; the
LaunchAgent plist is generated on apply (not stowed), since it needs the
absolute repo path baked in. Secrets read by the watcher must live outside
~/.config/<tool>/ (which is a Stow symlink back into the repo) — use
~/.config/dotfiles/ instead.

▌ D11: ctrl-cmd as AeroSpace modifier (2026-04) — superseded by D23

▎ Superseded by D23 (2026-05). AeroSpace removed. Rectangle ships with
▎ ctrl-cmd-<key> defaults that match this allocation, so the muscle memory
▎ carries over.

Decision: All AeroSpace bindings use ctrl-cmd-<key> as the prefix. Replaces an
earlier Hyper (cmd-ctrl-alt-shift-) scheme that relied on Karabiner's caps→Hyper
remap.

Why: Matches existing Rectangle muscle memory (Rectangle defaults to
ctrl-cmd-arrows for snap, ctrl-cmd-letters for corners/thirds). Hyper's
4-modifier safety isn't needed for this binding count. Hyper had a real gotcha:
any Hyper combo containing Cmd+Shift+/ (i.e. Hyper+/) is swallowed by macOS's
global Help-menu shortcut before AeroSpace sees it, so symbol-key bindings
silently failed. ctrl-cmd avoids that.

Tradeoffs and remediations:

• ctrl-cmd-F overrode macOS's Toggle-Fullscreen system shortcut. AeroSpace uses
  F as a workspace; system fullscreen replaced by AeroSpace's fullscreen on
  ctrl-cmd-Enter. Workspace renamed F → P (see D12).
• ctrl-cmd-Space is the macOS emoji picker. Float toggle moved off Space to
  ctrl-cmd-G.
• Layout toggles ctrl-cmd-/ and ctrl-cmd-, work cleanly under ctrl-cmd (they
  only failed under Hyper because of the Help-menu path).
• Karabiner caps→Hyper rule remains installed but unused by AeroSpace bindings;
  caps-tap → Esc still works for vim users.

▌ D12: Workspace P replaces F (2026-04) — superseded by D23

▎ Superseded by D23 (2026-05). Letter-named workspaces are gone with AeroSpace;
▎ macOS Spaces don't have user-defined letter labels.

Decision: Renamed the wide-monitor "free" workspace from F to P.

Why: With ctrl-cmd as the AeroSpace prefix (D11), ctrl-cmd-F is macOS's global
"Toggle Fullscreen" shortcut. Binding workspace F would override that system
fullscreen behavior. P is a free letter, no system or app conflict.

How to apply: Anywhere setup-guide examples or future docs mention a "Free /
TBD" workspace, use P. The M (meeting) and S (scratch) workspaces are unchanged.
Auto-place rules and persistent-workspaces list both use P now.

▌ D13: Accordion is keyboard-tabs only — no i3-style stacked layout (2026-04) —
  superseded by D23

▎ Superseded by D23 (2026-05). No tiling, no accordion. macOS Spaces + Rectangle
▎ replaced the whole layer.

Decision: Accept that AeroSpace's accordion layout is a keyboard-navigated stack
(ctrl-cmd-h/j/k/l cycles through siblings) and not an i3-style visual stacked
layout with title bars on each member of the stack.

Why: macOS apps enforce internal minimum window-size constraints that AeroSpace
can't override. With accordion-padding=80, AeroSpace requests inactive windows
be 80px tall; Chrome/Slack/Wavebox refuse, render at their min-size (~200px+)
and overlap the focused window instead of collapsing to a 28-30px title-bar
strip. Result: only one window appears visible. Some apps (terminals with no
min-size) can produce the strip effect, but not the typical mixed-app workspace.

Alternatives evaluated and rejected:

• Custom Übersicht widget for vertical tab strip (~80 LOC HTML/JS, but WebKit
  overhead per widget; and JS poll model is awkward fit).
• Custom Swift NSPanel sidebar (~300 LOC + ongoing maintenance for a single-user
  tool).
• Two SketchyBar instances (top + bottom horizontal) with column-aligned tab
  markers — markers can't be position-aligned with floating-point precision to
  actual columns; layout requires runtime spacer math.
• yabai's stack layout (works) — explicitly rejected by D7 because it requires
  disabling SIP.

How to apply: Use accordion as a "show only the focused window of this stack"
mode; navigate with focus keys. Don't try to make it look like i3 tabs. If
permanent visual tab strip becomes essential later, revisit Übersicht (cheapest
reversible path).

Future direction (2026-04): Two paths were evaluated for fixing the visual side
of accordion on macOS:

1. Standalone NSPanel-overlay helper alongside AeroSpace. Either a tab-stack
   strip (one bar at the top, click to focus a sibling, hidden siblings are
   pre-shifted down by 28px so the strip doesn't cover content) or a
   true-accordion mode (N title-bar NSPanels, one per sibling, with the focused
   window resized to fit between its title and the next; non-focused siblings
   parked off-screen). MVP cost: ~200 LOC for tabs / ~550 LOC for accordion.
   Rejected for now: payoff too narrow given AeroSpace already handles accordion
   functionally; per-switch latency in true-accordion mode (~50–200ms) is worse
   than AeroSpace's instant focus cycle.

2. Custom Mac-native WM — a "Rectangle with a brain" replacement: lets macOS own
   Spaces, tiles within the focused Space, ships its own bar (SketchyBar
   replacement) and overlays (drag-to-zone, peek hints, the tab-stack/accordion
   strips above). Cost: ~3–6 months solo, half-time. Trades AeroSpace's
   programmatic Spaces control for richer overlay UI (a real loss for
   cross-Space move-node-to-workspace-style features, a real gain for everything
   D13 punted on).

Neither is being built now. Revisit (2) if/when AeroSpace's limits become the
bottleneck again, or if the overlay UI becomes a clear must-have. (1) is
dominated by (2) — only build (1) if you decide against (2) and need the visual
cue badly enough to ship a 200-LOC helper.

▌ D14: VM dev architecture — cmux + helix layered (2026-04, revised 2026-05,
  further revised by D23)

▎ Further revised by D23 (2026-05). The "Mac WM" row is no longer AeroSpace —
▎ see D23. VM-side agent orchestration is provided by workslate (on-VM,
▎ tmux-based) — see docs/workslate-overview.md and D23.

Decision: For VM-based development, the tool stack is layered with each tool
doing one job:

┌────────────┬────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Layer      │ Tool                   │ Role                                   │
╞════════════╪════════════════════════╪════════════════════════════════════════╡
│ Mac WM     │ AeroSpace              │ Workspace + window tiling on Mac       │
├────────────┼────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Mac        │ cmux                   │ Vertical tabs + SSH-with-cmuxd, one    │
│ terminal   │                        │ window per scope/project; persistent   │
│ app        │                        │ panes survive cmux disconnects         │
├────────────┼────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ VM         │ shells inside cmux     │                                        │
│ execution  │ panes                  │                                        │
├────────────┼────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ VM editor  │ helix (default); nvim  │                                        │
│            │ or Zed via Remote-SSH  │                                        │
│            │ as escape hatches      │                                        │
└────────────┴────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────┘

Why: Each tool genuinely does one thing well. cmux owns the host-side
multiplexing and per-scope window grouping; helix is the default editor inside
any pane, fast to start and friendly to short edit loops. Zed via Remote-SSH
(NOT SSHFS — file-sync issues) is the escape hatch for occasional heavy
refactor; not a daily driver because spinning up multiple Zed windows per
worktree is inefficient.

Mapping to AeroSpace workspaces: one cmux window per scope (Work1, Work2,
Personal, etc.). Inside, vertical tabs for sessions.

Revised 2026-05 — tmux dropped from the user-facing layered stack: The original
2026-04 design included tmux as a user-facing layer for long-lived shell
sessions. In practice, cmux's persistent SSH panes already cover the
long-lived-session role at the user-facing level, so the user-facing tmux layer
(.tmux.conf, sesh session picker, manual prefix + workflows) was removed.

tmux is still required, just not user-facing: workslate (D23) uses tmux as its
on-VM substrate for agent panes and per-project layouts. Without tmux on the VM,
workslate won't start. The package therefore stays in vm/install.sh's
APT_PACKAGES as a workslate runtime dependency — not as a fallback for raw ssh,
which is a bonus side effect.

How to apply: Treat tmux as an internal dependency of workslate, not something
the user interacts with directly. Don't add a managed .tmux.conf or
session-picker tooling — anything you'd want there belongs in cmux config (the
primary user-facing multiplexer) or workslate's own configuration instead.

▌ D15: Stay on Chrome despite vertical-tabs envy (2026-04)

Decision: Chrome remains the primary browser. Vertical-tab workflows managed via
in-browser extensions (Link Map for tree-style tab management) rather than
switching to Edge/Vivaldi/Brave/Arc.

Why: Several tools are Chrome-locked or Chrome-best:

• "Claude in Chrome" extension and related Anthropic browser MCP
• Passbolt (and most password managers' best integration)
• Corporate SSO extensions that team-policy targets Chrome only
• Chrome-specific debugging APIs used by some dev tooling

The accumulated Chrome-only surface is too large to swap browsers cleanly.
Edge/Brave can install Chrome Web Store extensions but Anthropic and other
vendors only test the Chrome path; subtle bugs surface at the worst moment.

Alternatives evaluated and rejected:

• Arc: in maintenance mode (The Browser Company pivoted to Dia).
• Edge / Vivaldi / Brave: native vertical tabs but break Chrome-only tooling.
• Wavebox: heavy (Electron + per-app sandboxes) and paid for the workspace
  features.

Workaround for tab-management itch: Link Map extension (free, Chrome Web Store,
IndexedDB-backed). Tree-style tab manager that bridges "open tabs" and
"saved-for-later" in a unified vertical sidebar. Reduces the need for many
Chrome windows per scope — most workspaces collapse to one Chrome window with a
rich Link Map tree.

▌ D16: Shell tooling baseline for VMs (2026-04)

Decision: Every VM gets a baseline of modern CLI tools, installed via the host
distro's package manager (apt on Ubuntu).

Tier 1 — install on every VM:

┌─────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Tool            │ Replaces / adds                                            │
╞═════════════════╪════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╡
│ tmux            │ Terminal multiplexer — runtime dependency of workslate (it │
│                 │ manages tmux sessions per project on the VM). Not          │
│                 │ user-facing; cmux is the primary multiplexer per D14       │
│                 │ revision.                                                  │
├─────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ fzf             │ Fuzzy finder, used by many other tools                     │
├─────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ zoxide          │ Smart cd (frecency-based jump)                             │
├─────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ starship        │ Cross-shell prompt (fast, scope-aware)                     │
├─────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ eza             │ ls (colors, git, tree mode)                                │
├─────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ bat             │ cat (syntax highlighting, paging)                          │
├─────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ fd              │ find (sane defaults, fast)                                 │
├─────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ ripgrep (rg)    │ grep -r (much faster)                                      │
├─────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ delta           │ git diff pager (side-by-side, syntax)                      │
├─────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ atuin           │ Shell history (encrypted, searchable, syncable)            │
├─────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ lazygit         │ Git TUI (also handles git worktrees natively via w key)    │
├─────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ tealdeer (tldr) │ man (cheatsheet examples)                                  │
└─────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

Tier 2 — install when actually needed:

mise (language version manager), direnv (per-dir env), just (task runner), jq/yq
(JSON/YAML), httpie/xh (HTTP debugging), bottom (htop replacement), dust (du),
duf (df), yazi (TUI file manager), glow (markdown viewer).

zsh plugins (manual sourcing, no oh-my-zsh):

┌─────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Plugin                  │ Effect                                       │
╞═════════════════════════╪══════════════════════════════════════════════╡
│ zsh-autosuggestions     │ Inline grey suggestions from history         │
│ zsh-syntax-highlighting │ Real-time syntax check                       │
│ fzf-tab                 │ Replaces tab-completion menu with fzf picker │
└─────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────┘

Why: This is the consensus stack among CLI-heavy developers. Each tool earns its
place every day; the alternatives ("install only what you need") guarantee
you'll repeatedly hit a missing tool at a bad moment.

How to apply: When vm/base/install.sh is wired, install Tier 1 via apt + a
one-shot fetch for the few tools not packaged (sesh, atuin if not in repo). Tier
2 stays manual — install per-VM as the use case appears.

▌ D17: cmux layouts deferred until usage patterns emerge (2026-04)

Decision: Don't write cmux.json layouts yet. Reserve a future Stow package at
mac/config/cmux/cmux.json (Stows to ~/.config/cmux/) for when concrete per-scope
layouts become obvious from daily use.

Why defer: cmux supports per-workspace custom-command layouts via a JSON schema
documented at https://cmux.com/docs/custom-commands. Conceptually they fit the
scope model perfectly — one command per codename launches that scope's canonical
pane/surface arrangement (SSH into the VM, open an editor in a split, optionally
a browser surface, etc.). But the exact shape of each scope's layout depends on
real workflow patterns we haven't lived yet:

• Whether each scope wants 2-column or 3-column inside its cmux window
• Whether the agent pane shares with the editor or stays on its own
• Whether a browser surface is part of the default layout or only opened on
  demand
• Whether per-project subdirectories want their own sub-layouts

Writing the JSON before those patterns settle just guarantees rewrites.

How to apply: Once a scope has been used for ~a week and the layout
crystallizes, add an entry to mac/config/cmux/cmux.json with that scope's
preferred shape (workspace name, color, cwd, layout tree of panes/surfaces,
optional shortcut). Bind a hotkey or trigger from cmux's command palette. See
cmux.com/docs/custom-commands for the schema.

Layouts can also live per-repo at <repo>/.cmux/cmux.json — useful for
project-specific shapes that shouldn't apply globally.

Beware: keybindings inside cmux.json collide with AeroSpace bindings when cmux
is focused. Pick distinct ones (e.g. Cmd+Shift+<digit>) unless you want the
in-cmux shortcut to differ from outside.

▌ D18: Mac shell tooling baseline (2026-04)

Decision: Mac host gets a small shell-tooling baseline mirroring a subset of the
VM Tier 1 (D16): fd, bat, git-delta, eza, xh. These extend the existing
stow/git/jq line in the Brewfile.

Why these five:

• fd: find replacement with saner defaults; pairs with rg and fzf. Earns its
  slot daily.
• bat: cat with syntax highlighting; also serves as the preview backend other
  tools (fzf, delta) plug into.
• git-delta: best-in-class diff pager; one-time wire-up in ~/.gitconfig and
  forget. Pairs with bat.
• eza: ls replacement with git/tree integration. Aliases land in shell config
  when that file is added (not yet — see "How to apply").
• xh: Rust httpie. Faster + lighter than httpie; strictly better for the
  one-shot HTTP-debug case. Picked over posting (TUI client, overlaps with xh)
  and over httpie itself.

Rejected for now:

• pass: GPG-keyed CLI password store — conflicts with the existing direction
  toward 1Password CLI / Keychain (see top-level CLAUDE.md Secrets section).
  Don't add a third secret store.
• grex: regex generator; cute but used twice a year. Install ad-hoc when needed,
  not as a baseline.
• thefuck: typo-retry shell hook adds 100–300ms to startup for what fzf history
  + atuin already cover. Hard skip.
• posting: TUI HTTP client; overlaps with xh. Pick xh for one-shots, reach for
  Insomnia/Bruno-class tools if saved-collection workflow ever becomes a real
  need.

Deferred (kept for later):

• jrnl: plain-text journal CLI. Interesting as a stable, scriptable capture
  interface that AI agents could append to (unlike Apple Notes, which is
  intentionally human-only — see aerospace.toml Apple Notes → float rule). Worth
  installing only if committed to a single capture target file — most likely
  inside the Obsidian vault so jrnl entries show up alongside hand-written notes
  on workspace R, with optional shell function j for instant capture and a Zed
  task for longer entries. Skip until the capture-target question is settled;
  otherwise it fragments alongside Obsidian quick-capture and adds nothing.
• cbonsai: ASCII bonsai tree generator. Pure aesthetic, no workflow value.
  Install only if you decide you want a clear; cbonsai -l vibe-boot on new tabs.

How to apply: After brew bundle installs the new tools, wire them in:

1. delta — add to ~/.gitconfig (Stowed): [core] pager = delta, [interactive]
   diffFilter = delta --color-only, [delta] navigate = true, side-by-side =
   true.
2. eza — defer aliases until a Mac shell-config Stow package exists (no
   mac/config/zsh/ yet). When added, mirror VM aliases: ls=eza, ll=eza -lah
   --git --group-directories-first, tree=eza --tree --git-ignore. Keep the Mac
   and VM lists in sync.
3. fd, bat, xh — no config; install and use.

The VM-side baseline (D16) already includes these. This ADR only covers the Mac
host so cross-environment muscle memory stays consistent.

▌ D19: Mac shell config package + Tier-1 parity (2026-04)

Decision: The Mac host gains a Stow-managed shell-config package at
mac/config/zsh/ plus the rest of the Tier-1 CLI tools from D16, so muscle memory
between Mac and VMs converges. New tools added to the Brewfile: ripgrep, fzf,
zoxide, lazygit, starship, atuin, yq, bottom, dust, zsh-autosuggestions,
zsh-syntax-highlighting. fzf-tab is cloned (no brew formula).

Layout:


 mac/config/zsh/
   .zshrc          Stow-symlinked to ~/.config/zsh/.zshrc
   aliases.zsh     sourced from .zshrc
   .gitignore      ignores plugins/, local.zsh, .zsh_history
   plugins/        fzf-tab cloned here at install time (gitignored)


ZDOTDIR mechanics: zsh reads ~/.zshenv first, before any other config. We use
that file to set ZDOTDIR=$HOME/.config/zsh so the rest of the config lives under
~/.config/ and is Stow-managed like every other tool. ~/.zshenv cannot be
Stow-symlinked — it must live at $HOME, but the existing mac/config/ package
targets ~/.config/. Rather than introduce a second Stow target tree,
mac/install.sh writes the one ZDOTDIR line into ~/.zshenv idempotently.

Plugin order: completion (compinit) first, then fzf-tab, then
zsh-autosuggestions, then zsh-syntax-highlighting last. Syntax highlighting must
be the final source — it wraps the line editor and will miss prior-defined
widgets if it loads earlier. fzf-tab must come after compinit (it intercepts the
menu) and before syntax-highlighting.

Why curated, not oh-my-zsh / zinit / zplug: plugin managers add ~100ms to shell
startup and a maintenance surface for features we don't need. Three sourced
files + brew-installed plugins do everything a plugin manager would, faster,
with less to break.

Local overrides: ~/.config/zsh/local.zsh is gitignored and sourced last.
Per-machine path tweaks, secret env vars, and one-off aliases belong there — not
in the committed .zshrc.

Rejected for now:

• tmux on Mac: cmux already provides session/tabs. Install if you ever start a
  Mac terminal outside cmux; otherwise unused.
• mise / direnv: defer until language-versioned dev work moves onto the Mac host
  (currently it lives on VMs).
• tealdeer (tldr): nice-to-have, not yet daily.
• yazi / superfile: TUI file manager. Skipped because Finder + cmux shell handle
  the 95% case. If a need surfaces, prefer yazi — vim-style keys match the rest
  of the stack (helix, cmux), Lua plugin layer is genuinely useful, and async
  I/O makes it noticeably faster on large dirs. superfile wins only if you
  specifically want Finder-feel TUI; it lacks a real plugin layer and is
  newer/smaller community.

How to apply (existing machine):


 brew bundle install --file=mac/Brewfile   # pulls the new tools
 ./mac/install.sh                          # writes ~/.zshenv, clones fzf-tab
 ./mac/restow.sh                           # links ~/.config/zsh
 exec zsh                                  # reload shell
 atuin login                               # if you want history sync


The next time the VM-side stack diverges from D16's Tier-1 list, update this
ADR's parity claim explicitly — don't let the two drift silently.

▌ D20: Keyboard shortcut allocation policy (2026-04)

Decision: Each layer of the Mac/VM stack owns a distinct modifier flavor. New
bindings must respect the allocation; don't add a binding in a layer that's
already claimed by a layer above it.

▌ Layered capture order

A keystroke is consumed at the topmost layer that binds it. Anything lower in
the stack never sees a key the layer above already caught.


 keystroke
    ↓
 [1] macOS system    Cmd+Tab, Ctrl+Cmd+F (fullscreen), Ctrl+Cmd+Q (lock),
                     Cmd+Shift+Q (logout), Cmd+Shift+3/4/5 (screenshot),
                     Ctrl+arrows / Ctrl+1..9 (Spaces — disabled by default)
    ↓
 [2] AeroSpace       ctrl+cmd+*, ctrl+cmd+alt+*, alt+shift+*
                     (see mac/config/aerospace/aerospace.toml)
    ↓
 [3] cmux app        cmd+*, cmd+shift+*, alt+cmd+* (selected),
                     ctrl+1..4, ctrl+alt+cmd+. (palette)
    ↓ SSH passthrough — only what cmux didn't catch reaches the VM
    ↓
 [4] tmux            Ctrl+B (prefix) + chord
    ↓
 [5] helix / nvim    plain letters, ctrl+letter, alt+letter,
                     Space (helix leader)


▌ Modifier-flavor allocation

┌─────────────────┬─────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────┐
│ Modifier        │ Owner                   │ Notes                            │
╞═════════════════╪═════════════════════════╪══════════════════════════════════╡
│ ctrl+cmd+*      │ AeroSpace               │ Window-manager actions; never    │
│                 │                         │ bind in cmux/VM.                 │
├─────────────────┼─────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────┤
│ ctrl+cmd+alt+*  │ AeroSpace               │ Window-move + resize; never bind │
│                 │                         │ elsewhere.                       │
├─────────────────┼─────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────┤
│ alt+shift+*     │ AeroSpace               │ Globally consumed; never bind in │
│                 │ move-node-to-workspace  │ cmux or VM tools.                │
├─────────────────┼─────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────┤
│ cmd+*           │ cmux app (when focused) │ Per-app; doesn't reach VM.       │
│                 │ + macOS                 │                                  │
├─────────────────┼─────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────┤
│ cmd+shift+*     │ cmux app + macOS        │ Per-app; doesn't reach VM.       │
├─────────────────┼─────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────┤
│ alt+cmd+*       │ cmux app (selected      │ cmux uses many but not all —     │
│                 │ combos)                 │ alt+cmd+w/]/[ are free and used  │
│                 │                         │ by D20's remap.                  │
├─────────────────┼─────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────┤
│ ctrl+letter     │ tmux + editors (passes  │ Safe in VM tools; macOS rarely   │
│                 │ through)                │ binds plain ctrl+letter.         │
├─────────────────┼─────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────┤
│ alt+letter      │ tmux + editors (passes  │ cmux's macos-option-as-alt =     │
│                 │ through)                │ true keeps these usable.         │
├─────────────────┼─────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────┤
│ Plain letters / │ editors                 │ Always pass through.             │
│ digits          │                         │                                  │
└─────────────────┴─────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────┘

▌ "Do not bind" rules

When configuring a tool inside the VM (tmux, helix, nvim), do not use:

• Ctrl+Cmd+* — AeroSpace eats it before cmux/SSH ever see it.
• Alt+Shift+* (especially digits and the workspace letters P/M/S/C/R/A/O) —
  AeroSpace globally consumes for move-node-to-workspace. Particularly tempting
  for tmux window-switch bindings; resist.
• Cmd+* — never reaches the VM (cmux/macOS catch). If tempted, this belongs in
  cmux's settings, not in the VM tool's config.

When configuring cmux, do not use Ctrl+Cmd+* for any new binding — it'll
silently never fire because AeroSpace claims it first.

▌ Confirmed safe modifier flavors per layer

For new bindings in each tool, use these:

• AeroSpace: stick with ctrl+cmd+* family; don't migrate to alt+shift+* for new
  actions (that's reserved for window moves).
• cmux: prefer alt+cmd+* (most combos free) or cmd+shift+* (most combos free).
  Avoid ctrl+cmd+*.
• tmux: stick with Ctrl+B prefix + ASCII chord. If you need a no-prefix bind,
  plain Ctrl+letter works (provided macOS doesn't reserve the specific letter —
  Ctrl+F, Ctrl+Cmd+F, etc.).
• helix / nvim: defaults are all safe. Plugin authors who use Cmd+P etc. won't
  work over SSH; use the editor's leader-key conventions (Space p / <leader>p)
  instead.

▌ Concrete remaps (this rule's first application)

cmux defaults conflicted with AeroSpace bindings on three keys. Overrides
committed at mac/config/cmux/settings.json:

┌───────────────────────────────────────┬──────────────┬───────────┐
│ Action                                │ cmux default │ Override  │
╞═══════════════════════════════════════╪══════════════╪═══════════╡
│ Close window (closeWindow)            │ cmd+ctrl+w   │ cmd+opt+w │
│ Next sidebar tab (nextSidebarTab)     │ cmd+ctrl+]   │ cmd+opt+] │
│ Previous sidebar tab (prevSidebarTab) │ cmd+ctrl+[   │ cmd+opt+[ │
└───────────────────────────────────────┴──────────────┴───────────┘

Ctrl+Cmd+F (cmux's toggleFullScreen) was left alone — it overlaps with macOS
system Toggle-Fullscreen but cmux's full-screen wins inside its own window, and
the user accepted that semantics.

How to apply (when changing cmux bindings):

1. Edit mac/config/cmux/settings.json (Stowed to ~/.config/cmux/settings.json).
2. Quit + relaunch cmux. cmux reads the file at startup only.

How to apply (when adding new AeroSpace bindings):

1. Edit mac/config/aerospace/aerospace.toml.
2. aerospace reload-config — picks up immediately, no restart.

▌ D21: Split Ghostty configs for Ghostty.app vs cmux (TODO, not urgent)

Problem: Ghostty.app and cmux both read ~/.config/ghostty/config, so they share
background-opacity and background-blur. cmux appears to ignore background-blur,
so the same background-opacity = 0.7 makes cmux's terminal pane look noticeably
darker than Ghostty.app's (unblurred dark wallpaper bleeds through directly in
cmux).

Deferred plan (when this becomes annoying enough to fix):


 mac/config/ghostty/
   config-base       # everything shared: theme, palette, fonts, padding,
                     # keybinds, font-family, cursor, clipboard, etc.
   config            # \`config-file = config-base\` + Ghostty-only:
                     #   background-opacity = 0.7
                     #   background-blur = 20
   config-cmux       # \`config-file = config-base\` + cmux-tuned:
                     #   background-opacity = 0.85   (no blur to compensate)


• ~/.config/ghostty/config (Stow target — cmux reads here) → points at
  config-cmux.
• ~/Library/Application Support/com.mitchellh.ghostty/config (Ghostty.app reads
  here, currently mirrored from ~/.config/ghostty/ by mac/apply.sh) → repointed
  at config directly.

Each app gets its own opacity; cmux compensates for the missing blur by going
more opaque. ~30 LOC of edits across the three files plus a small apply.sh
tweak.

Not blocking: the current single-config setup is acceptable; the visual mismatch
is real but not painful. Revisit if/when cmux either adds blur support (problem
disappears) or you find yourself using cmux heavily and the dimness becomes a
daily irritant.

▌ D22: SSH access to OrbStack VMs (2026-04)

▎ Note (2026-05): the crane scope mentioned throughout the examples below was
▎ retired. Active code/config now uses tux / beaver / otter / owl / hawk. The
▎ recipes in this ADR are still correct — just substitute the current scope list
▎ when re-running them.

Decision: Address every OrbStack VM as ssh <codename> from the Mac host, routed
through OrbStack's SSH bridge. SSH config uses Match exec "orb info %n" to
auto-detect any running OrbStack VM, with no need to maintain a per-VM list.

▌ Why bridge over .orb.local

OrbStack exposes VMs through two transports:

┌─────────────────┬─────────────────┬───────────────────────┬──────────────────┐
│ Path            │ Transport       │ Auth source           │ Survives VM      │
│                 │                 │                       │ recreation?      │
╞═════════════════╪═════════════════╪═══════════════════════╪══════════════════╡
│ ssh <vm>@orb    │ OrbStack's      │ ~/.orbstack/ssh/id_   │ ✓                │
│ (or via Host    │ local           │ ed25519               │                  │
│ orb block)      │ helper-binary   │ (OrbStack-managed,    │                  │
│                 │ FD-pass proxy   │ auto-deposited in     │                  │
│                 │                 │ every VM)             │                  │
├─────────────────┼─────────────────┼───────────────────────┼──────────────────┤
│ ssh             │ Plain network   │ ~/.ssh/id_* from your │ ✗ — fresh VMs    │
│ <vm>.orb.local  │ SSH against the │ regular keys, must be │ have empty       │
│                 │ VM's IP         │ in the VM's           │ authorized_keys  │
│                 │                 │ /home/<user>/.ssh/    │                  │
│                 │                 │ authorized_keys       │                  │
└─────────────────┴─────────────────┴───────────────────────┴──────────────────┘

The bridge path is strictly better: OrbStack maintains its key chain across VM
lifecycles, and you never touch a VM's authorized_keys. The .orb.local path
"works the first time" only when an old VM had a manual ssh-copy-id; recreate it
and .orb.local fails with publickey-denied while the bridge keeps working
untouched. Standardize on the bridge.

▌ Codename-as-identity (relates to ADR-D14)

VM hostnames mirror the AeroSpace workspace codenames: crane (work), owl
(financial), hawk (real estate), beaver (personal-dev), otter (low-touch
personal). This makes ctrl-cmd-1 → workspace 1 → ssh crane → crane VM all carry
the same identity through every layer (workspace → cmux window → VM hostname →
SSH alias). No mental translation step.

▌ The SSH config block

Lives at the end of ~/.ssh/config. OrbStack's auto-injected Include
~/.orbstack/ssh/config is at the top of the file (added by OrbStack on install —
leave it alone).

                                                                      sshconfig
 # OrbStack VM auto-routing — \`ssh <name>\` works for any running
 # OrbStack VM. Match exec runs \`orb info <name>\`; if it succeeds (the
 # VM exists), the OrbStack bridge directives below apply. Non-OrbStack
 # hosts fall through to other config blocks normally.
 Match originalhost * exec "orb info %n >/dev/null 2>&1"
     User %n
     Hostname 127.0.0.1
     Port 32222
     IdentityFile ~/.orbstack/ssh/id_ed25519
     IdentitiesOnly yes
     UserKnownHostsFile ~/.orbstack/ssh/known_hosts
     ProxyCommand '/Applications/OrbStack.app/Contents/Frameworks/OrbStack Help
↪er.app/Contents/MacOS/OrbStack Helper' ssh-proxy-fdpass 501
     ProxyUseFdpass yes
     StrictHostKeyChecking accept-new
     ServerAliveInterval 30
     ForwardAgent yes


The Hostname/Port/IdentityFile/ProxyCommand quartet is copied verbatim from
OrbStack's Host orb block in ~/.orbstack/ssh/config — that's the actual
transport. We replicate it because SSH config doesn't re-evaluate Host blocks
after a HostName substitution, so chaining through HostName orb (the naive
approach) silently fails.

▌ Per-scope SSH keys + agent forwarding

Decision: all private keys live on Mac only. Each AeroSpace scope (workspace
codename) gets its own ed25519 key:


 ~/.ssh/id_ed25519_tux       — Mac itself / personal-life
 ~/.ssh/id_ed25519_beaver    — personal-dev VM
 ~/.ssh/id_ed25519_otter     — low-touch personal VM
 ~/.ssh/id_ed25519_crane     — work VM
 ~/.ssh/id_ed25519_owl       — financial VM
 ~/.ssh/id_ed25519_hawk      — real-estate VM


Generation (one-shot for all six):

                                                                             sh
 for scope in tux beaver otter crane owl hawk; do
     ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -N '' \\
         -f ~/.ssh/id_ed25519_$scope \\
         -C "remzi $scope $(date +%Y-%m-%d)"
     ssh-add --apple-use-keychain ~/.ssh/id_ed25519_$scope
 done


--apple-use-keychain stores the (empty) passphrase in macOS Keychain so
ssh-agent auto-loads all keys at login.

ForwardAgent yes in the routing block above forwards the Mac's ssh-agent socket
into every VM session. Inside a VM, ssh-add -l shows every key the Mac holds,
available for signing. Private keys never copy to a VM. A compromised or
destroyed VM loses nothing — the canonical store is Mac, backed up via Time
Machine.

Trade-off: a session-active VM with root access could use the forwarded agent to
authenticate as you to other services until you log out. Acceptable for local
OrbStack VMs you trust; do not enable forwarding for untrusted remote hosts.

▌ Per-VM authorized_keys (currently dormant)

Each VM's /home/remzi/.ssh/authorized_keys has been pre-seeded with its matching
scope pubkey (id_ed25519_<scope>.pub). This file is not used today because
OrbStack VMs don't run sshd listening on port 22 — the bridge handles all auth
via the helper-binary FD-pass. The deposited keys sit dormant; they activate the
moment you ever enable sshd on a VM (sudo systemctl enable --now ssh), at which
point direct .orb.local SSH would also work.

Pre-seeding script (run once per VM if recreating):

                                                                             sh
 for scope in beaver otter crane owl hawk; do
     cat ~/.ssh/id_ed25519_\${scope}.pub | ssh \${scope}@orb \\
       'mkdir -p ~/.ssh && cat >> ~/.ssh/authorized_keys && chmod 700 ~/.ssh &&
↪ chmod 600 ~/.ssh/authorized_keys'
 done


▌ Three lessons hard-won during this setup

1. Use %n, not %h, for User in dynamic blocks. %h resolves to the
   post-substitution Hostname (here 127.0.0.1), which makes OrbStack route to
   whatever the "default" container is — usually the first one created —
   regardless of which alias you typed. %n is always the
   original-hostname-as-typed, which is what we want. Symptom of getting this
   wrong: ssh crane lands in the otter VM.

2. Match originalhost * exec "<probe>" auto-discovers VMs. The probe runs once
   per SSH invocation. If orb info <name> exits 0, the block applies. If
   non-zero (no such VM), the block is skipped and SSH falls through to other
   rules. This means new VMs work immediately with zero config edits, and
   non-OrbStack hosts (github.com, etc.) still route through their own dedicated
   blocks. Per-SSH cost is one local RPC (~10–30ms); negligible.

3. Host orb block ≠ HostName orb. OrbStack's Host orb directive contains a
   ProxyCommand that talks to a helper binary via FD-pass — it's not literally a
   TCP connection to a host called "orb". Setting HostName orb in any other
   block doesn't trigger re-evaluation of that block; SSH just tries to
   DNS-resolve "orb" and fails. If you want the bridge in another alias, copy
   the actual directives, don't chain.

▌ Maintenance caveats

• The ProxyCommand path is hardcoded. If OrbStack ever moves OrbStack Helper.app
  or rebrands, the literal path breaks. Recovery: re-run cat
  ~/.orbstack/ssh/config and copy the new ProxyCommand verbatim into the block.
• The 501 argument is the macOS UID. Confirm yours with id -u and adjust if not
  501.
• orb must be in PATH when SSH evaluates Match exec. It is for interactive
  shells. If running ssh from a stripped environment that lacks it, replace orb
  with the absolute path to the binary (which orb shows the location).

▌ Why this is not a Stow package

The literal ~/.ssh/config contains machine-specific paths (OrbStack
helper-binary location, user UID) and pre-existing hand-edited blocks (GitHub
keys, Colima includes). Tracking it verbatim would cause merge friction on a
fresh Mac without saving work. Re-deriving the file from this ADR is a 30-second
copy-paste, which is faster than maintaining a Stow package with per-machine
patches.

▌ Future enhancement — helix "stack-and-flush" AI clipboard helper

Pending design (worth implementing once helix is committed as the only VM
editor): keybinds that accumulate path:line references and labeled snippets into
~/.cache/ai-clipboard.md, then flush the whole stack to the clipboard in one
paste for a CC/OC prompt that references multiple files/chunks.

Rough keybind shape:

• space y l → append path:line for cursor
• space y p → append path only
• space y c → append the selection with a --- path:line --- header
• space y v → view current stack
• space y f → flush stack to clipboard, clear file
• space y x → clear without flushing

VM-side variant uses OSC 52 escape instead of pbcopy so cmux relays clipboard
back to Mac. Works around helix's missing per-selection line-number variables
(multi-cursor on its own can't yank N path:line refs because %{cursor_line} only
resolves the primary selection's line). Stack-based design is editor-agnostic —
same shape works in nvim if helix ever proves restrictive for this case.

Not blocking; the simpler space y l plus content yank covers most prompts.
Promote to live config when the multi-ref case bites regularly.

▌ Fresh-Mac runbook (single-paste recovery)

Bootstrapping a new Mac to the working state captured by this ADR:

                                                                             sh
 # 1. Install OrbStack — populates ~/.orbstack/ssh/config and adds the
 #    \`Include ~/.orbstack/ssh/config\` line at the top of ~/.ssh/config.
 brew install --cask orbstack

 # 2. Append the auto-routing block to ~/.ssh/config.
 mkdir -p ~/.ssh
 cat >> ~/.ssh/config <<'EOF'

 # OrbStack VM auto-routing — \`ssh <name>\` works for any running
 # OrbStack VM. See ADR-D22 in docs/decisions.md.
 Match originalhost * exec "orb info %n >/dev/null 2>&1"
     User %n
     Hostname 127.0.0.1
     Port 32222
     IdentityFile ~/.orbstack/ssh/id_ed25519
     IdentitiesOnly yes
     UserKnownHostsFile ~/.orbstack/ssh/known_hosts
     ProxyCommand '/Applications/OrbStack.app/Contents/Frameworks/OrbStack Help
↪er.app/Contents/MacOS/OrbStack Helper' ssh-proxy-fdpass 501
     ProxyUseFdpass yes
     StrictHostKeyChecking accept-new
     ServerAliveInterval 30
     ForwardAgent yes
 EOF
 chmod 600 ~/.ssh/config

 # 3. Generate one ed25519 key per scope; load each into Keychain.
 for scope in tux beaver otter crane owl hawk; do
     ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -N '' \\
         -f ~/.ssh/id_ed25519_$scope \\
         -C "remzi $scope $(date +%Y-%m-%d)"
     ssh-add --apple-use-keychain ~/.ssh/id_ed25519_$scope
 done

 # 4. (Optional) Pre-seed authorized_keys on each VM you've created.
 #    Skip this if VMs don't exist yet — do it after creating each one.
 for scope in beaver otter crane owl hawk; do
     cat ~/.ssh/id_ed25519_\${scope}.pub | ssh \${scope}@orb \\
       'mkdir -p ~/.ssh && cat >> ~/.ssh/authorized_keys && chmod 700 ~/.ssh &&
↪ chmod 600 ~/.ssh/authorized_keys'
 done

 # 5. Smoke test.
 for scope in beaver crane hawk otter owl; do
     ssh -o BatchMode=yes \${scope} 'echo authenticated' \\
         && echo "✓ \${scope}" || echo "✗ \${scope}"
 done

 # 6. Upload pubkeys to the matching GitHub accounts (manual, web UI).
 #    GitHub → Settings → SSH and GPG keys → New SSH key.
 #    Title format: <scope>-<YYYY-MM>, e.g. crane-2026-04.
 for scope in tux beaver otter crane owl hawk; do
     echo "--- $scope ---"
     cat ~/.ssh/id_ed25519_\${scope}.pub
 done


Total time on a fresh Mac with OrbStack already installed: under a minute for
the SSH side. Adding the GitHub pubkeys is whatever your copy-paste rhythm
allows.

▌ D23: Drop AeroSpace — macOS-native + Rectangle + Wavebox + cmux + workslate
  (2026-05)

Decision: Replace the AeroSpace-based Mac WM stack with:

• Mac windowing — macOS Spaces + Mission Control for workspace switching;
  Rectangle for keyboard-driven window placement (halves / quarters / thirds).
  No tiling WM, no on-window-detected rules.
• Browser identity — Wavebox as primary, with per-scope app groups providing the
  per-context split that Chrome's profile-per- scope was attempting. The
  chrome-scope helper stays as a fallback (see mac/docs/chrome-profiles.md).
• VM access — cmux unchanged (one window per scope, persistent SSH panes).
• VM agent orchestration — workslate (on the VM, tmux-based) manages per-project
  panes, worker agents, and worktrees. See docs/workslate-overview.md.
• Task tracking — worklog (markdown-file-based CLI + TUI).

Why:

• AeroSpace's on-window-detected rules depend on window titles, which many of
  the apps actually in use here don't expose stably (cmux per SSH host, Chrome
  per profile suffix, Wavebox, OrbStack, Ghostty). When a workspace's rule
  didn't match, restarts would consolidate the unmatched windows on workspace 0
  — hit twice in a 24-hour stretch with phantom windows.
• macOS Spaces handle the multi-display + multi-context split natively and
  survive reboots without ruleset upkeep. They lose programmatic cross-Space
  window movement (a real cost), but for daily flow that was rarely worth the
  rule-maintenance overhead.
• Rectangle's defaults overlap heavily with the muscle memory built up under D11
  (ctrl-cmd-<key>), so the keyboard layer was a near-zero-cost transition.

What was archived, then deleted (2026-09-25):

mac/archive/aerospace/ held the config, helpers, SketchyBar plugins and full
setup guide. Removed once it was clear AeroSpace was not coming back — it had
sat unused for four months, and the linter was reporting 127 issues in a setup
guide nobody would follow again.

The reasoning is what mattered and it lives here, in this ADR and in D7 and D11.
The files are recoverable from git history if ever needed:

                                                                             sh
 git log --all --diff-filter=D -- mac/archive/aerospace   # find the deletion
 git show 7285618:mac/archive/aerospace/config/aerospace.toml
 git checkout 7285618 -- mac/archive/aerospace              # restore the tree


7285618 is the last commit that contains it.

What's removed from the active stack:

• mac/config/aerospace/ (moved to archive)
• mac/bin/aerospace-* (moved to archive; .swift sources only — compiled binaries
  dropped)
• cask "aerospace" from mac/Brewfile; replaced with cask "rectangle".
• mac/docs/aerospace-setup-guide.md (moved to archive)
• AeroSpace + Karabiner mentions in mac/switchover.sh TOOLS, mac/install.sh
  post-install steps, mac/apply.sh reload section, mac/macos-defaults.sh
  comment, mac/README.md stack list, and per-folder CLAUDE.md notes.
• Workspace pills + layout-status pills in sketchybarrc. The bar is now
  status-only (clock, compute, memory, audio, camera, OBS).

Supersedes: D7, D11, D12, D13 (all AeroSpace-specific). Further revises: D14 (VM
dev architecture — "Mac WM" row no longer AeroSpace; workslate provides on-VM
agent orchestration).

How to apply:

• Window placement: macOS-native Spaces (Mission Control settings → set up
  per-scope Spaces, optionally pin Wavebox / Slack / Obsidian / OBS to specific
  Spaces). Rectangle hotkeys for placement within a Space. No rules to maintain.
• Per-scope browser context: prefer Wavebox app groups. If a tool is
  Chrome-locked (Claude in Chrome, corporate SSO extensions — see D15), use
  chrome-scope <scope> from mac/local/bin/.
• Per-VM agent flow: cmux SSH into the VM, then workslate for agent
  orchestration; worklog for task tracking. tmux remains as the substrate
  workslate uses on the VM, not user-facing.
• New ADRs that touch window placement or per-scope context should reference D23
  rather than D7/D11/D12/D13.

▌ D24: Launcher-driven Space placement via Hammerspoon (2026-05-10)

Decision: Route per-scope windows to specific macOS Desktops via
switch-then-launch — synthesize the system Ctrl+N Space-switch hotkey via
Hammerspoon, then let the launcher's open / cmux new-window create the window on
the now-active Desktop. After-the-fact window movement
(hs.spaces.moveWindowToSpace) is not used; that API is broken on macOS Sequoia
(Hammerspoon issue #3698
<https://github.com/Hammerspoon/hammerspoon/issues/3698> — the function returns
success but doesn't actually move windows). The pivot to keystroke-based
switching was forced by that regression.

Components:

• mac/hammerspoon/.hammerspoon/init.lua — owns the scope→Desktop mapping (single
  source of truth, single screen for now) and exposes scope.gotoScopeSpace(name)
  over hs.ipc. Implementation: move cursor to primary screen center (so the
  symbolic-hotkey dispatcher targets primary) → send Ctrl+N. Also exposes
  scope.closeAllScopeWindows() for the post-restart fixup flow.
• mac/local/bin/chrome-scope — Chrome profile launcher. Calls hs -c
  "scope.gotoScopeSpace('<scope>')", sleeps briefly, then open -na "Google
  Chrome" --profile-directory=…. Chrome's new window is born on the now-active
  Desktop. Works from anywhere (terminal, Raycast).
• mac/local/bin/cmux-scope — cmux per-scope window launcher. Same
  switch-then-create flow, plus cmux CLI calls to name the new window and seed
  an SSH workspace. Only fully functional from inside a cmux-managed terminal
  (cmux's CLI authenticates via Unix peer-credentials check; outside cmux, cmux
  ping fails with "Broken pipe"). From Raycast / non-cmux shells, the script
  bails early after the Space switch and leaves window creation to the user via
  cmux's UI.
• mac/local/bin/cmux-rebootstrap — post-restart fixup. cmux's restore-session
  brings windows back across quit/relaunch cycles but always on Desktop 1 (macOS
  doesn't persist per-window Space assignment for cmux). Since we can't move
  them, this script recreates each scope on its target Desktop via cmux-scope
  <scope>. Misplaced restored windows on Desktop 1 are left for the user to
  close manually (closing them automatically destabilizes cmux's
  window-placement anchor and breaks the create loop).
• mac/local/share/scope-routing/ — 14 Raycast script-command wrappers (7 scopes
  × 2 tools). Stowed to ~/.local/share/scope-routing/; Raycast's Settings →
  Extensions → Script Commands is configured to scan that directory.

Why this design (after the original moveWindowToSpace plan failed):

• Ctrl+N is dispatched by WindowServer directly (symbolic-hotkey layer),
  bypassing the broken CGSConnection path. Reliable.
• "Switch first, then create" sidesteps the problem entirely — the window is
  born on the right Desktop, no movement needed.
• Window-finding (for "focus existing" cases) uses Hammerspoon's cross-Space
  window enumeration filtered by macOS title and bundle ID. Titles are ones we
  set via cmux rename-window, so the matching is deterministic (this is not the
  title-fragility D23 rejected — that was about matching titles we don't
  control).

Hard limitations baked into the design:

┌────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────┐
│ Limitation             │ Cause                   │ Workaround                │
╞════════════════════════╪═════════════════════════╪═══════════════════════════╡
│ hs.spaces.             │ Hammerspoon issue #3698 │ Switch-then-launch        │
│ moveWindowToSpace      │ / Apple's CGSConnection │ instead of                │
│ doesn't move windows   │ restrictions on Sequoia │ launch-then-move          │
├────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────┤
│ hs.spaces.gotoSpace    │ Same                    │ Synthesize Ctrl+N hotkey  │
│ doesn't switch Spaces  │                         │ via hs.eventtap.keyStroke │
├────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────┤
│ cmux CLI fails outside │ cmux's socket auth via  │ cmux-scope detects        │
│ cmux-managed shells    │ process attribution; no │ $CMUX_SOCKET_PATH and     │
│ (Broken pipe)          │ readable token in any   │ bails early outside cmux. │
│                        │ settings/keychain file  │ Raycast invocations only  │
│                        │                         │ do the Space switch +     │
│                        │                         │ bring cmux forward        │
├────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────┤
│ cmux's restore-session │ macOS                   │ cmux-rebootstrap closes   │
│ loses per-window       │ NSWindowRestoration in  │ restored windows,         │
│ Desktop assignment     │ cmux saves window state │ recreates fresh on right  │
│                        │ but not Space ID        │ Desktops via crex (full   │
│                        │                         │ state) or cmux-scope      │
│                        │                         │ (minimal)                 │
├────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────┤
│ cmux's session JSON    │ cmux schema only saves  │ Accept the limitation;    │
│ doesn't preserve       │ CWDs and structure      │ user re-runs ssh <vm>     │
│ startup commands or    │                         │ inside each window after  │
│ SSH targets            │                         │ rebootstrap               │
├────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────┤
│ AI session continuity  │ cmux doesn't track      │ Use each agent's native   │
│ (claude/opencode/codex │ agent session IDs       │ --resume flag manually;   │
│ resume across cmux     │                         │ out of scope for our      │
│ restarts)              │                         │ routing                   │
├────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────┤
│ Ctrl+N targets the     │ macOS symbolic-hotkey   │ Move cursor to primary    │
│ focused screen, not    │ dispatcher uses the     │ before sending the        │
│ always primary         │ focused window's screen │ keystroke. Note:          │
│                        │                         │ secondary-screen-focused  │
│                        │                         │ terminals may still       │
│                        │                         │ misroute — run cmux-scope │
│                        │                         │ from primary              │
├────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────┤
│ Yabai would solve all  │ Requires SIP-disable    │ Rejected per ADR-D7       │
│ of the above           │                         │ (security cost)           │
└────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────┘

Why not Yabai or hs._asm.undocumented.spaces:

┌───────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Option                        │ Verdict                                      │
╞═══════════════════════════════╪══════════════════════════════════════════════╡
│ Yabai                         │ Rejected — SIP-disable. Same blocker as D7.  │
├───────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Native Automator / Shortcuts  │ No public API for window→Space movement.     │
├───────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ AppleScript via System Events │ assigned to space is private. Mission        │
│                               │ Control UI scripting is slow and fragile.    │
├───────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ hs._asm.undocumented.spaces   │ Predecessor of hs.spaces. Same underlying    │
│                               │ private CGSConnection API; almost certainly  │
│                               │ broken in the same way on Sequoia. Skipped   │
│                               │ without testing.                             │
├───────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Hammerspoon                   │ Public, reliable. The actual mechanism we    │
│ hs.eventtap.keyStroke ✅      │ ship.                                        │
└───────────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────┘

Defaults shipped (single screen, main monitor):


 Desktop 1 → tux       (personal / dotfiles)
 Desktop 2 → beaver    (workslate / worklog dev)
 Desktop 3 → owl
 Desktop 4 → hawk      (ESO)
 Desktop 5 → otter
 Desktop 6 → other     (catch-all)


Secondary screen left unmapped intentionally — fewer Spaces and the
single-screen flow covers the actual use cases. Extend later if a real routing
case appears.

Required user setup (first-time per machine):

1. brew bundle install (adds cask "hammerspoon" to Brewfile).
2. ./mac/restow.sh (stows init.lua to ~/.hammerspoon/, wrappers to
   ~/.local/bin/, Raycast scripts to ~/.local/share/scope-routing/).
3. Open Hammerspoon once → grant Accessibility permission.
4. From the Hammerspoon console once: hs.ipc.cliInstall() (needs admin).
5. Create 7 macOS Desktops on the main monitor in Mission Control.
6. Enable "Switch to Desktop 1–6" hotkeys in System Settings → Keyboard →
   Keyboard Shortcuts → Mission Control.
7. Add ~/.local/share/scope-routing/ to Raycast's Script Commands directory
   list.
8. From a cmux terminal, run the bootstrap loop once to create the per-scope
   cmux windows.

Operating workflow:

• Daily: chrome-scope <scope> or Raycast chrome: <scope> works from anywhere —
  Chrome window appears on the right Desktop. cmux navigation: cmux-scope
  <scope> from inside cmux works fully; from outside cmux, only the Space switch
  happens.
• After cmux quits + restarts: run cmux-rebootstrap from a cmux terminal. It
  recreates each scope window on its target Desktop via cmux-scope.
  Inside-window content (running ssh sessions, shells, processes) is not
  restored — the user re-establishes per-window state manually.

Composes with: D23 (macOS-native + Rectangle + Wavebox stack); D15 (Chrome stays
primary for tools that need it). Doesn't supersede anything — adds a routing
layer on top of D23 without contradicting any of its choices.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

▌ D25: Documentation categories — ideas, plans, and a tools register
  (2026-09-25)

Decision: Split repo documentation into four kinds rather than three, and scope
the README-per-directory rule to exclude Stow payload.

┌─────────────────────┬────────────────────────┬───────────────────┐
│ Kind                │ Where                  │ Kept current?     │
╞═════════════════════╪════════════════════════╪═══════════════════╡
│ Standing references │ docs/*.md              │ yes               │
│ Decision log        │ docs/decisions.md      │ yes, append-only  │
│ Candidate register  │ docs/possible-tools.md │ yes               │
│ Captured scope      │ docs/ideas/            │ dated, not frozen │
│ Planned work        │ docs/plans/            │ dated, not frozen │
└─────────────────────┴────────────────────────┴───────────────────┘

▌ Why docs/ideas/ separate from docs/plans/

docs/plans/ held two documents that open with Status: **parked** and are
organised as threads with next steps. A design that has been thought through but
has no threads and no next steps does not fit that shape — calling it a plan
implies a commitment that was never made.

So: ideas are what this would be and why; plans are what to do about it. An idea
graduates by getting a plan rather than becoming one: the idea keeps the design
and the ruled-out options, the plan takes the work breakdown and the sequencing,
and a pointer joins them. Neither kind is frozen — a parked document stays as
its snapshot, one under active discussion is updated in place, and the Status:
line says which.

The trigger was the slipbox design, which lived only in Claude's memory store —
the wrong home for something meant to be re-read cold months later.

▌ Why docs/possible-tools.md is a register, not an idea

Tools that were evaluated but not adopted kept accumulating as memory notes:
hcom, slk, lazyrsync, hygg, the agent-plus-notes reference repos, and leaf
before it was adopted. Each carried real work — install routes, maturity
cautions, the specific questions to answer before committing.

That is not an idea, because it never graduates into a plan; it is a standing
register that keeps gaining entries. It is the waiting room for this file: a
candidate sits there until installed, then earns a mac/Brewfile line and an ADR
here if the choice mattered. Rejected candidates stay, with the reason, so they
are not re-evaluated from scratch.

▌ Why the README rule now exempts Stow payload

CLAUDE.md said "Every directory should have a README.md". Followed literally
inside a Stow package, that scatters symlinked READMEs through $HOME.

GNU Stow 2.4.1's built-in ignore list contains ^/README.*, but anchored to the
package root. So vm/base/README.md is skipped while
vm/base/.config/git/README.md would be symlinked to ~/.config/git/README.md on
every VM. Verified with a dry run against a throwaway package: the top-level
README was ignored, the nested one was linked.

The rule now applies to non-payload directories — docs/, docs/plans/, vm/docs/,
vm/lib/, vm/scopes/ — and stops at the payload boundary. Documentation for a
package's contents belongs in its top-level README or in the relevant docs/
folder. Existing practice already agreed: no .config/<tool> directory in this
repo has a README.

Composes with: D14 (codename-as-identity, for the scope docs); the
curated-Brewfile rule in CLAUDE.md, which possible-tools.md feeds. Supersedes
nothing — it subdivides an existing convention and narrows one rule that was
overbroad."
`;

exports[`snapshots of the layout IR as text links-and-unicode.md @ 80 1`] = `
"── ≡ properties ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 title    Links and unicode
 aliases  link fixture, wikilink sample
 tags     fixture, synthetic
 created  2026-09-25
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

▌ Links and unicode

Synthetic fixture. Unlike its siblings in this folder, this file was written
rather than copied — nothing in the source repository contained a single
wikilink, so the feature slipbox exists for had no test material at all.

▌ Wikilinks

A plain link to wide-tables and one to a note that does not exist,
nonexistent-note, which should be marked unresolved rather than silently
rendered.

A piped link reading the dense table sample, and one with surrounding
punctuation (wrap-probe), which is where naive regex matching usually breaks.

A heading reference, large-document › D25: Documentation categories, and a block
reference, long-form-mixed ^thread-one.

An embed follows, and should transclude rather than link:

⧉ wrap-probe

Links that must not be links:

                                                                           text
 [[this is inside a fence]] and ![[so is this]]


Inline code with [[brackets]] in it, and an escaped [[literal]].

A standard markdown link to the README, a bare autolink
https://example.invalid/path, and a reference-style link
<https://example.invalid/reference>.

▌ Display width

These rows all contain the same number of characters and different display
widths, which is the difference that breaks column alignment:

┌───────────┬──────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Kind      │ Sample           │ Notes                                │
╞═══════════╪══════════════════╪══════════════════════════════════════╡
│ ASCII     │ abcdefgh         │ 8 columns                            │
│ CJK       │ 日本語のテキスト │ 8 characters, 16 columns             │
│ Korean    │ 한국어의 텍스트  │ mixed with a space                   │
│ Emoji     │ 🌍🚀📝🔥🎯🧪📊🛠  │ 8 characters, 16 columns             │
│ ZWJ       │ 👨‍👩‍👧‍👦               │ one grapheme, several code points    │
│ Combining │ é vs é           │ precomposed vs e + U+0301            │
│ RTL       │ مرحبا بالعالم    │ right-to-left run inside an LTR line │
└───────────┴──────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────┘

A paragraph mixing scripts so wrapping has to segment correctly rather than
counting bytes: これは日本語のテキストで、ASCII と混ざっています。한국어도 여기
있습니다. Plus an emoji 🎯 and a family 👨‍👩‍👧‍👦 that must stay whole when a line
breaks near it.

▌ Ordinary structure

• A list item
  ◦ nested, with a wide-tables link inside it
  ◦ and inline code
☐ an unchecked task
☑ a checked task

▎ A blockquote with bold, italic, strikethrough and a wrap-probe link.
▎
▎ ▎ Nested one level deeper.

Term-style content, a footnote reference[1], and a horizontal rule.

1. The footnote body.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

▌ Edge cases worth keeping

An empty table cell, a pipe escaped inside a cell, and a very long unbreakable
token:

┌──────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Column       │ Value                                                         │
╞══════════════╪═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╡
│ empty next   │                                                               │
├──────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ escaped pipe │ a | b                                                         │
├──────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ unbreakable  │ aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa │
│              │ aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa                                           │
└──────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

A heading immediately followed by a table with no blank line between them is
malformed on purpose — the parser should not crash on it."
`;

exports[`snapshots of the layout IR as text long-form-mixed.md @ 80 1`] = `
"▌ Parked plan: nvim ↔ agent integration

Status: parked — to revisit. Captured 2026-06-10 during a longer exploration of
where the stack should go next.

Five threads live here. They're related but can be picked up independently:

1. agentic.nvim for multi-agent ACP chat sharing sessions with terminal CLIs
   (Claude / Codex / Gemini / OpenCode).
2. LintaoAmons/bookmarks.nvim for line-level notes with a <leader>aw "wrap up
   and deliver to agent" hook.
3. Herdr pane-tabs feature request (or fork) to make multi-agent layouts viable
   without eating screen estate.
4. Herdr socket sender from nvim — small lua module wrapping herdr pane
   send_text / send_keys; underpins Thread 2's delivery channel.
5. Herdr layout: Scenario A chosen — 3-column at 2/3 Ghostty, escape-hatch
   ladder for breathing room. Most awaited primitives (pane split --ratio, pane
   resize --amount, pane swap, pane focus, pane zoom) landed in v0.6.10 stable —
   fleet upgraded, herdr-3col rewritten to use float ratios directly. Only pane
   layout save / apply still outstanding for future v0.7+.

The full conversation context lives in this session's transcript at
/Users/remzi/.claude/projects/-Users-remzi-Projects-dotfiles/2748c705-70a5-42ea-
8b72-8589a904be82.jsonl.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

▌ Thread 1 — agentic.nvim

▌ Why this and not something else

• Built around Zed's Agent Client Protocol (ACP). Pure ACP chat client; no
  legacy API adapters cluttering the config.
• Sessions interchange with terminal CLIs: a Claude session started in a Herdr
  pane is resumable from nvim and vice versa, because ACP providers manage
  sessions natively (Claude/Codex/ Gemini/OpenCode all implement session/list).
• Newest in the space (active commit cadence into Jun 2026), pure Lua, no
  lazy.nvim lock-in → installs cleanly via vim.pack on kickstart.nvim.
• Complements Herdr instead of duplicating it: Herdr stays the terminal-side
  multiplexer, agentic.nvim is the chat front-end inside nvim. Same agent
  backend, two views.

Alternative we considered: olimorris/codecompanion.nvim. Heavier, more mature,
supports both ACP and MCP, has chat/inline/agent modes and MCPHub integration.
Pick CodeCompanion only if we later want MCP servers as slash commands;
otherwise agentic is the right "do one thing well" choice for this profile.

▌ Setup requirements (on each VM where we run agents)

Install ACP wrappers per agent:

                                                                             sh
 npm install -g acp-claude-code     # Claude Code → ACP
 npm install -g codex-acp            # Codex → ACP
 # gemini-cli already supports --experimental-acp natively
 npm install -g auggie-acp           # Augment Code → ACP (optional)


Then agentic.nvim config (rough sketch — finalize on pilot):

                                                                            lua
 require('agentic').setup({
   providers = {
     claude = { command = 'acp-claude-code' },
     codex  = { command = 'codex-acp' },
     gemini = { command = 'gemini-cli', args = { '--experimental-acp' } },
   },
 })


▌ Open questions to validate during pilot

• Does it work cleanly with vim.pack on kickstart.nvim (no lazy.nvim)? Almost
  certainly yes — pure Lua — but verify.
• Can it discover a Claude session running in a Herdr pane via session/list
  without manual session-ID copy? Likely yes for Claude (writes session JSONL
  under ~/.claude/projects/) but worth confirming the lookup mechanism.
• How does the chat UI render in narrow splits — does it stay usable with nvim
  split + chat panel side-by-side, or want a full split?

▌ When we resume

Pilot it on hawk:

1. Install acp-claude-code via npm
2. Add carlos-algms/agentic.nvim to nvim plugins (vim.pack)
3. Start a claude session in a Herdr pane on hawk
4. Open nvim in a different pane, open agentic.nvim chat
5. Verify session list shows the running Herdr-pane session
6. Resume it from nvim, send the current selection — see if the reply lands in
   both views

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

▌ Thread 2 — LintaoAmons/bookmarks.nvim

▎ DROPPED 2026-09-22. Never installed or wired. prompt-buffer.lua already covers
▎ the adjacent need — capturing path:42-50 refs into a per-project scratch — and
▎ the annotation use case never became pressing enough to justify four open
▎ decisions plus a Thread 4 dependency. The analysis below is kept because the
▎ plugin comparison is still accurate if this is ever reconsidered.

▌ Why this one specifically

Other bookmark plugins (tomasky/bookmarks.nvim, crusj/bookmarks.nvim,
chentoast/marks.nvim, etc.) are either outdated or single-list.
LintaoAmons/bookmarks.nvim is the multi-list bookmark plugin — one collection
per task / topic / agent-conversation. That maps cleanly to "wrap up this list
of notes and deliver to an agent."

Each bookmark: file + line + description + list. JSON-persisted,
telescope-pickable. Active list switchable from the picker.

▌ Decisions pending (need user input before wiring)

▌ Decision 1 — Keybindings

Two options:

┌────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────┐
│ Style                  │ Marks own m-prefix                │ <leader>m* only │
╞════════════════════════╪═══════════════════════════════════╪═════════════════╡
│ mm add                 │ sacrifices a native vim-mark      │ safer, slower   │
│                        │ letter, faster muscle memory      │                 │
├────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────┤
│ mi add with annotation │                                   │                 │
├────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────┤
│ mx delete              │                                   │                 │
├────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────┤
│ <leader>ml list active │ shared                            │ shared          │
├────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────┤
│ <leader>mL switch list │ shared                            │ shared          │
├────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────┤
│ <leader>mc create list │ shared                            │ shared          │
├────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────┤
│ <leader>md delete list │ shared                            │ shared          │
├────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────┤
│ <leader>mr rename list │ shared                            │ shared          │
└────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────┴─────────────────┘

User TBD.

▌ Decision 2 — Sign-column style

Emoji 🔖 vs text glyph (▶ plain, ◆ annotated)?

User TBD.

▌ Decision 3 — Wrap-up content (the <leader>aw bundle)

What goes into the markdown bundle delivered to an agent?

Levels:

• Minimum: bookmarks (file:line + annotation) only
• + snippets: 3 lines of context around each bookmark
• + scratch buffer: a parked free-form note buffer
• + visual selection: current selection appended
• + git diff since list was created

User TBD. Suggest: bookmarks + 3-line snippets as v1; layer in scratch/selection
later.

▌ Decision 4 — Delivery channel

One key, three keys, or configurable?

• <leader>aw → Herdr socket → agent pane (one-shot delivery)
• <leader>aW → save to /tmp/session-notes-<ts>.md (manual reference)
• <leader>aY → pbcopy / system clipboard

User TBD. Suggest: three-keybind variants for flexibility.

▌ Storage strategy (decided)

• Local per-VM (default ~/.local/share/nvim/bookmarks/bookmarks.json).
• NOT synced via dotfiles — bookmarks are session/task data, not config. Plugin
  config lives in dotfiles; bookmarks JSON stays local per machine.
• List names are free-form per task (not auto-by-branch); revisit later if
  manual create+switch feels heavy.

▌ Proposed wrap-up output format

                                                                       markdown
 # Session notes: auth-refactor

 ## Bookmarks

 ### \`auth/middleware.ts:42\`
 > token decode looks wrong — should bail if header missing
 \`\`\`ts
     const token = req.headers.authorization?.split(' ')[1];
     const decoded = jwt.verify(token, SECRET);  // ← throws on null
     req.user = decoded;


▌ auth/routes.ts:18

▎ unprotected route, intentional?

                                                                             ts
     router.post('/refresh', refreshHandler);


▌ Free-form notes

(scratch buffer content if any)



 ### When we resume

 1. Lock in the four decisions above
 2. Add \`LintaoAmons/bookmarks.nvim\` to
    \`vm/base/.config/nvim/lua/custom/plugins/\`
 3. Wire keybinds + signs + telescope picker
 4. Write the \`<leader>aw\` wrap-up function (stub first — save to
    file; later add Herdr-socket delivery once Thread 4 lands)
 5. Stow + sync to all VMs

 ---

 ## Thread 3 — Herdr pane-tabs feature

 ### The problem

 Herdr's current pane layout is split-only. To run nvim + multiple
 agents side-by-side, you split into N panes — which eats screen
 real estate quickly (4 panes = tiny). What we want is
 **browser-style tabs *within* a pane region**: nvim takes the left
 half, the right half is a single pane slot that tabs between
 \`claude\`, \`codex\`, \`gemini\`.

 This isn't natively supported. Workspace-level tabs in Herdr swap
 **entire layouts**, not single pane slots.

 ### Three paths

 | Path | Effort | Outcome |
 | --- | --- | --- |
 | **A. File feature request** at \`github.com/ogulcancelik/herdr\` | 15 min | Ma
↪intainer may accept; defers the decision |
 | **B. Workaround via agentic.nvim** | Already covered by Thread 1 | nvim host
↪s a single chat panel that swaps between agents; no need for Herdr pane-tabs s
↪ince ACP routes to multiple agents from one nvim panel |
 | **C. Fork Herdr and add it** | Significant — Rust, layout engine, UI, socket
↪ API | Best learning + upstream PR but real time cost |

 ### Recommendation when we resume

 1. **File the issue first** (Path A). Cheap, may unblock.
 2. **Lean on Thread 1 (\`agentic.nvim\`)** for multi-agent UX since
    ACP routing from one nvim panel sidesteps the Herdr pane-tabs
    need entirely.
 3. **Defer the fork decision** until weeks of real use show whether
    pane-tabs are still missing.

 ### Issue draft (rough text for filing)

 > **Feature request: tabbed pane groups (browser-style tabs within a pane regi
↪on)**
 >
 > Currently Herdr panes can only be split. For workflows that
 > involve one persistent editor (nvim) + multiple agent CLIs
 > (claude, codex, gemini) accessed one at a time, splits eat screen
 > estate quickly.
 >
 > Request: a "tabbed pane group" — a pane slot that holds N
 > processes with a tab strip, only one visible at a time, like
 > browser tabs.
 >
 > Use case: left half = nvim. Right half = tabbed group with
 > claude / codex / gemini. Switch agents without losing nvim's
 > space.
 >
 > Workspace-level tabs in Herdr already swap full layouts. This
 > feature is the more granular version at the pane-group level.

 ---

 ## Thread 4 — Herdr socket sender from nvim

 ### What it is

 A small nvim lua module that uses \`herdr pane send_text\` and
 \`herdr pane send_keys\` to deliver text from nvim to a running agent
 in a Herdr pane. Underpins Thread 2's \`<leader>aw\` delivery
 channel, and is independently useful for "send selection to
 agent" workflows even without bookmarks.

 ### Sketch

 \`\`\`lua
 -- ~/.config/nvim/lua/custom/herdr-send.lua
 local M = {}

 local function get_agent_pane(agent_name)
   local raw = vim.fn.systemlist('herdr agent list --json')
   local list = vim.fn.json_decode(table.concat(raw, '\\n'))
   -- Pick the first pane labeled <agent_name>, or first agent if none
   for _, a in ipairs(list) do
     if a.agent == agent_name then return a.pane_id end
   end
   return list[1] and list[1].pane_id or nil
 end

 function M.send(text, opts)
   opts = opts or {}
   local pane = opts.pane or get_agent_pane(opts.agent or 'claude')
   if not pane then
     vim.notify('No agent pane found', vim.log.levels.WARN)
     return
   end
   vim.fn.system({'herdr','pane','send_text',pane,text})
   vim.fn.system({'herdr','pane','send_keys',pane,'Return'})
 end

 return M


▌ Default keybinds (proposal)

┌─────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Key                 │ Action                              │
╞═════════════════════╪═════════════════════════════════════╡
│ <leader>sc (v-mode) │ Send selection to first claude pane │
│ <leader>sx (v-mode) │ Send selection to first codex pane  │
│ <leader>sg (v-mode) │ Send selection to first gemini pane │
│ <leader>sp          │ Pick target pane interactively      │
└─────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────┘

▌ When we resume

Lightweight; can land independently of Threads 1–3.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

▌ Thread 5 — Herdr layout: Scenario A chosen

▌ What landed

After exploring two layouts at length, settled on Scenario A:


 [ Wavebox 1/3 ]   [ Ghostty 2/3 — sidebar 22 + 3-column content ]
                     │ col1 main agent ~140c │ col2 nvim ~140c │ col3 others ~7
↪5c │


• Wavebox at 1/3 screen — most web apps render fine at this width (Slack,
  GitHub, Jira, Linear, ChatGPT, etc.). Exceptions: Canva/Figma (canvas-heavy) —
  handle case-by-case.
• Ghostty at 2/3 screen — content area ~365 cols after 22-col sidebar.
• One Ghostty tab per VM, each running hvm <vm> (the hvm helper sets the tab
  title to the VM name via OSC 2).
• Per project: one Herdr workspace, with the 3-column layout built via
  herdr-3col (in vm/base/.config/zsh/.zshrc).
• Per worktree: grouped child workspace under the source — same 3-column layout
  once herdr pane layout save/apply ships (currently a manual rebuild per
  worktree).

▌ Column purpose + ergonomic calibration

┌──────┬───────────────────────┬──────────────┬────────────────────────────────┐
│ Col  │ Role                  │ Target width │ Rationale                      │
╞══════╪═══════════════════════╪══════════════╪════════════════════════════════╡
│ col1 │ Main agent            │ ~140c        │ Closest to screen center (~46% │
│      │ (claude/codex/gemini  │              │ mid) — eye rest                │
│      │ TUI)                  │              │                                │
├──────┼───────────────────────┼──────────────┼────────────────────────────────┤
│ col2 │ nvim                  │ ~140c        │ Comfortable for single-buffer  │
│      │                       │              │ code at 120-char limit         │
├──────┼───────────────────────┼──────────────┼────────────────────────────────┤
│ col3 │ Others stack          │ ~75c         │ Narrow but sufficient for      │
│      │ (codex+gemini in      │              │ peripheral TUIs (>60c minimum) │
│      │ vertical sub-panes)   │              │                                │
└──────┴───────────────────────┴──────────────┴────────────────────────────────┘

The 120-char ergonomic limit is the calibration point. Going above ~140c per
column doesn't help readability — it just adds whitespace. Going below ~120c per
column tightens code below the comfort threshold.

Per-pane size measured via tput cols after the user's manual tune: ~121/121/54 —
close to target ratios; refinement deferred to v0.7+ when --ratio lands in
stable.

▌ Scenario B (alternative considered, rejected for now)

Scenario B = two Herdr workspace tabs per project:

• Tab 1: nvim alone (full Ghostty width)
• Tab 2: agents 2-column (main + others stack)

Math at 2/3 Ghostty: Tab 1 nvim gets 365c, Tab 2 main agent ~237c, others ~128c.
More breathing room everywhere; sidebar still rolls state across both tabs.
Would pair beautifully with agentic.nvim (Thread 1) since the chat panel could
split Tab 1 while Tab 2 hosts the native TUIs.

Rejected because:

• Nvim at 140c (Scenario A) already comfortable for single-buffer — the extra
  225c in Scenario B is mostly whitespace
• TUI agents at 140c already past their comfortable rendering — the extra 97c in
  Scenario B's main column is also whitespace
• Tab switching adds friction vs always-visible 3-column
• Less peripheral awareness of agent output (only sidebar; no live pane content
  visible)

Trigger to revisit B: if Tab 1 use cases regularly demand nvim + chat panel
split (each at 50%), OR if pane swap never ships and the "rotate active agent
into col1" workflow stays broken.

▌ Escape-hatch ladder for nvim breathing room

┌───────────────┬───────────────────┬────────────────────┬─────────────────────┐
│ Need          │ Action            │ Cost               │ Win                 │
╞═══════════════╪═══════════════════╪════════════════════╪═════════════════════╡
│ A little more │ prefix+b (close   │ Lose sidebar       │ +22c distributed    │
│ room          │ sidebar)          │ status visibility  │ across columns      │
├───────────────┼───────────────────┼────────────────────┼─────────────────────┤
│ Vsplit /      │ prefix+z (zoom    │ Hide agent panes   │ nvim gets full      │
│ IDE-style     │ nvim)             │ (sidebar still     │ ~365c → 182/182     │
│ layout        │                   │ works if open)     │ vsplit comfortable  │
├───────────────┼───────────────────┼────────────────────┼─────────────────────┤
│ Heavy vsplit  │ Dedicated nvim    │ Extra setup + 2    │ Always-available    │
│ daily         │ tab in workspace  │ nvim processes     │ wide nvim           │
└───────────────┴───────────────────┴────────────────────┴─────────────────────┘

Default flow stays at rung 0 (3-col + sidebar). Step up only when needed.
Dedicated nvim tab is parked as future option — only worth building if zoom
proves repetitive in actual use.

▌ Sub-decision: half-screen Ghostty alternative

Briefly considered: 1/2 Wavebox + 1/2 Ghostty. Math: 265c content, nvim 132/132
vsplit (at the 120 limit, tight). Rejected because Tab 1 in Scenario B doesn't
gain enough to compensate for tighter splits. 2/3 Ghostty wins for any layout
that involves nvim splits.

▌ Status update 2026-06-14 — most of these landed in v0.6.10 stable

What was supposedly "waiting on v0.7+" turned out to be in v0.6.10 stable (terse
release notes had hidden them — actual binary picked up the full
preview-promoted set). Fleet upgraded across the board:

• Mac (via brew upgrade), beaver/otter/owl (vendor herdr update), hawk (already
  there from the brief preview detour). All on v0.6.10 matching versions, so
  thin-client + image paste still work.
• pane split --ratio FLOAT → herdr-3col now uses 0.405 / 0.685 / 0.4 directly;
  lands at 121/121/54 on a 304-col Ghostty (target was 122/122/52). Close enough
  to call done.
• pane resize --amount FLOAT, pane swap, pane focus, pane zoom, pane layout,
  pane neighbor, pane edges — all available in stable now.
• Only pane layout save / apply (save current layout as named template, apply to
  a new workspace) doesn't appear in the v0.6.10 CLI yet — pane layout exists
  but takes only a pane id, not a save/apply subcommand. That's still
  preview-only / future work.

herdr-v07-release-watch scheduled task stays running — useful for catching pane
layout save / apply and whatever else lands in true v0.7+.

▌ Open questions for when we revisit

• Does prefix+z zoom preserve nvim's split state when unzooming? (Almost
  certainly yes — zoom is purely visual — but verify.)
• When sidebar is closed, do agent toast notifications still fire? Config has
  [ui.toast] delivery = "terminal" so Ghostty handles notifications via OSC;
  should be independent of sidebar visibility.
• Does the existing v0.6.8 sidebar_width = 22 setting actually apply on reload,
  or only on full server restart? Observed inconsistency on beaver — applied via
  server reload-config with no diagnostics but visible change wasn't obvious.
  Re-test on next fresh session.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

▌ Compose order (when we resume)

These threads layer naturally:

1. Thread 4 (Herdr socket sender) first — its stated purpose was Thread 2's
   delivery channel. With Thread 2 dropped it has no consumer; revisit only if
   something else needs to send text into a Herdr pane from nvim.
2. Thread 2 (bookmarks) — dropped 2026-09-22, see above.
3. Thread 1 (agentic.nvim) — fully independent, just an nvim plugin install +
   ACP wrappers. Now the first thing to build if this plan is picked back up.
4. Thread 3 (Herdr pane-tabs) — filed 2026-09-21 as a Discussions idea, not an
   issue: herdrdev/herdr runs a bugs-only issue policy and auto-closed the
   earlier #3775 with a pointer to Ideas.
5. Thread 5 (Herdr layout) — passive; triggered by the v0.7+ release watch task.
   When it fires, rewrite herdr-3col and evaluate pane layout save/apply as a
   replacement primitive.

Realistic chunk for one focused session: Thread 4 + Thread 2 wiring (stop before
the agent-delivery wiring). Then a separate session for Thread 1 pilot. Thread 3
is mostly off our plate after filing the issue. Thread 5 is hands-off until the
release watch alerts us."
`;

exports[`snapshots of the layout IR as text overlong-table-rows.md @ 80 1`] = `
"▌ workslate — workshop conventions for solo + agent work

A small system of tools and conventions for solo knowledge work with AI agents
as collaborators. Implements the VM-side agent orchestration role described in
ADR-D14 / ADR-D23.

This file is an overview + pointers. Authoritative design lives in each
project's own repo.

▌ Vocabulary

┌───────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Term      │ Meaning                                                          │
╞═══════════╪══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╡
│ workshop  │ The place. Top-level container, conventionally ~/workshop/.      │
├───────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ workbench │ A project. ~/workshop/<project>/.                                │
├───────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ worktree  │ A task thread. A git worktree on its own branch, including the   │
│           │ project's primary main/ checkout.                                │
├───────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ worker    │ An agent (claude / codex / opencode) running inside a worktree,  │
│           │ doing focused work.                                              │
├───────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ foreman   │ The user's maestro agent at the workshop level — orchestrates    │
│           │ work across workbenches.                                         │
├───────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ rig       │ CLI tool that prepares worktrees and lays out cmux panes.        │
├───────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ worklog   │ CLI tool for task tracking — markdown files in .tasks/           │
│           │ directories.                                                     │
└───────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

▌ Directory layout


 ~/workshop/
 ├── simpletodo/                       ← workbench (umbrella)
 │   ├── main/                         ← primary checkout (a worktree)
 │   ├── add-list/                     ← worktree on branch add-list
 │   ├── .worktree-setup.sh            ← optional setup hook
 │   └── .tasks/                       ← worklog task files
 │
 ├── dotfiles/
 │   └── …
 │
 └── .tasks/                           ← cross-workbench managerial tasks


~/.tasks/ (above the workshop) is also valid for personal / system-level items.

▌ Architecture

• foreman runs at the workshop level (one cmux ssh tab per scope, persistent).
  Has the broad context: every workbench, every .tasks/ directory, external
  integrations.
• worker runs at the worktree level (one cmux ssh tab per task, spawned by rig).
  Has narrow context: its task, its files, the relevant tdl subtree.
• foreman ↔ workers communicate only through worklog files — handoffs, comments,
  mentions, status changes. No in-process IPC, no message bus. That boundary
  keeps each agent's context clean.

  One alternative to this was flagged and never weighed: hcom, in
  docs/possible-tools.md. It is parked deliberately — the entry records what it
  would have to beat.

▌ Components

┌───────────┬──────────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────┐
│ Component │ Type                             │ Repo / location               │
╞═══════════╪══════════════════════════════════╪═══════════════════════════════╡
│ rig       │ bash CLI for worktree + cmux     │ own repo (separate from       │
│           │ orchestration                    │ dotfiles)                     │
├───────────┼──────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────┤
│ worklog   │ TypeScript-on-Bun CLI for task   │ own repo (separate from       │
│           │ tracking                         │ dotfiles)                     │
├───────────┼──────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────┤
│ foreman   │ agent role + skill files /       │ TBD — likely the future       │
│           │ CLAUDE.md fragments              │ workslate meta-repo           │
└───────────┴──────────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────┘

Each component is independently usable:

• worklog works without the rest — a standalone task tracker.
• rig works without worklog — just creates worktrees and lays out panes; if
  worklog is on $PATH, it integrates.
• A foreman is just a claude session at ~/workshop/ reading the conventions; the
  role exists even with neither tool.

▌ Where the design lives

The substantive design for each component is in its own repo's docs/design.md.
This file does not duplicate that content.

• rig — separate repo (delegated to another agent for implementation; design doc
  within it).
• worklog — separate repo (implemented; design doc within it).
• workslate as a meta-system (foreman behavior, install flow, cross-component
  conventions) — TBD; will get its own repo when that design phase begins.

▌ What lives in dotfiles

• This overview note.
• Future: workshop bootstrap glue — vm/install.sh additions that ensure the
  conventions are in place on a fresh VM (create ~/workshop/, install rig and
  worklog, place any shared skill files).

▌ Status (2026-05-03)

▌ Component status

