│ archive                │                            │ rig ships              │
├────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────┤
│ Clipboard relay to     │ cmux native selection (OSC │ in dotfiles, working   │
│ host                   │ 52)                        │                        │
├────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────┤
│ Task tracker           │ worklog                    │ implemented + verified │
└────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────┴────────────────────────┘

▌ Quality-of-life items — not blockers

┌────────────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Item                                   │ Workaround                          │
╞════════════════════════════════════════╪═════════════════════════════════════╡
│ Conversation history browser           │ claude --resume for current         │
│ (searchable, multi-agent history from  │ sessions; manual JSONL inspection   │
│ ~/.claude/projects/...)                │ for older state                     │
├────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Cross-worktree status dashboard (every │ cmux tab strip shows presence; rig  │
│ workspace's agent state at a glance)   │ status --branch X checks one at a   │
│                                        │ time                                │
└────────────────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────┘

▌ Real blockers — needed before flows can run

┌─────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────┐
│ Blocker     │ What it is                              │ Plan                 │
╞═════════════╪═════════════════════════════════════════╪══════════════════════╡
│ Worker      │ A document the agent reads on session   │ Drafting next;       │
│ CLAUDE.md / │ start telling it how to use worklog     │ ~200-line markdown   │
│ skill file  │ (call worklog context / worklog         │                      │
│             │ current, append worklog log notes,      │                      │
│             │ write worklog handoff before context    │                      │
│             │ fills, set worklog status review when   │                      │
│             │ done). Without it, a fresh claude       │                      │
│             │ session has no idea worklog exists.     │                      │
├─────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────┤
│ Foreman     │ Workshop-level orientation for the      │ Draft after first    │
│ CLAUDE.md / │ maestro agent: what's in ~/workshop/,   │ real flow run        │
│ skill file  │ how to delegate to workers via rig, how │ reveals what context │
│             │ to plan across workbenches, when to     │ the maestro actually │
│             │ create cross-cutting tasks vs           │ needs                │
│             │ workbench-local ones.                   │                      │
├─────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────┤
│ Workshop    │ Existing checkouts live at              │ Migrate one project  │
│ migration   │ ~/Projects/<proj>/; rig assumes         │ (probably tdl since  │
│             │ ~/workshop/<proj>/main/. Not migrated   │ it's most active) to │
│             │ yet.                                    │ validate; then the   │
│             │                                         │ others               │
├─────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────┤
│ Bootstrap   │ A fresh VM should land in workslate     │ Add to vm/install.sh │
│ convention  │ convention automatically — ~/workshop/  │ once steps 1–3 prove │
│             │ created, rig + worklog on PATH, skill   │ the convention       │
│             │ files placed. Currently implicit /      │                      │
│             │ by-hand for beaver.                     │                      │
└─────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────┴──────────────────────┘

▌ Smaller niceties (not on critical path)

• Approval helper. When claude pauses on [Y/n] you currently attach the agent's
  pane. A 5-line rig approve <branch> (cmux send-key y enter) shaves friction if
  it becomes annoying.
• Worklog prompts library. A templated first-prompt flow ("Begin Work on
  Ticket") via worklog context <slug> returning a context block. The worker
  skill file should tell the agent to read that on start.

▌ Smallest path to running flows

1. ✅ worklog implemented
2. ⏳ rig lands (delegated)
3. 🔲 worker CLAUDE.md skill file
4. 🔲 migrate one project (probably tdl → ~/workshop/tdl/main/) to validate
   Layout C
5. 🔲 run an end-to-end flow on that project: cmux ssh beaver --name test-task →
   rig new test-task <slug> → claude works → rig rm test-task → verify worklog
   archive
6. 🔲 foreman CLAUDE.md skill file (after step 5)
7. 🔲 vm/install.sh bootstrap additions (after step 5)

After step 5 the system is real and self-sustaining; everything below is
hardening and reach.

▌ History

This file was originally wt-cmux-design.md — an in-flight working note for the
worktree+cmux orchestrator (then called wt, now rig) and the task tracker (then
tdl, now worklog). Once each tool got its own repo and design doc, this note
lost its purpose. Trimmed to an overview + pointers on 2026-05-03."
`;

exports[`snapshots of the layout IR as text wide-tables.md @ 80 1`] = `
"▌ Markdown wrap + table render test

Test fixture for pipe_table.wrap in render-markdown v8.14.0+. Lines here are
deliberately long and WILL fail markdownlint — that is the point, ignore the
diagnostics.

Toggle wrap with <leader>tw and compare each section in both states.

▌ 1. Long prose — wrap should help here

This paragraph is one very long single line with no hard breaks in it at all,
which is exactly the case where soft wrapping earns its keep, because without
wrap you have to scroll horizontally to read a sentence that would otherwise fit
comfortably in the window if only the editor were allowed to break it at a word
boundary for you. With wrap off this runs off the right edge. With wrap on it
should break cleanly at word boundaries, and with linebreak also on it should
avoid breaking mid-word.

Short control paragraph. Nothing interesting should happen to this one.

▌ 2. Wide table, long cells — the main event

This is the case that forced nowrap. Each cell holds a full sentence.

┌───────────┬─────────────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────┐
│ Option    │ What it does                    │ When it bites you              │
╞═══════════╪═════════════════════════════════╪════════════════════════════════╡
│ pipe_     │ Fits wide tables to the current │ Only available from v8.14.0    │
│ table.    │ window by wrapping individual   │ onward, so an older pin        │
│ wrap      │ cells, replacing the source     │ silently gives you the old     │
│           │ lines with virtual lines on     │ horizontal-scroll behaviour    │
│           │ Neovim 0.11 and later.          │ with no warning at all.        │
├───────────┼─────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────┤
│ anti_     │ Controls whether the line under │ Set to false for kickstart     │
│ conceal.  │ the cursor stays rendered or    │ parity, which means the cursor │
│ enabled   │ reverts to raw source text      │ line keeps its rendering and   │
│           │ while you are sitting on it.    │ you never see the raw pipes    │
│           │                                 │ while editing a row.           │
├───────────┼─────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────┤
│ cell      │ Determines how individual cells │ padded and trimmed compute     │
│           │ are rendered — overlay, raw,    │ different widths, so switching │
│           │ padded, or trimmed — which      │ between them can change        │
│           │ changes how width is computed   │ whether a given table fits the │
│           │ for each column.                │ window at all.                 │
└───────────┴─────────────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────┘

▌ 3. Many columns, short cells — control case

┌────┬────────┬───────┬─────┬─────┬──────┬────────┐
│ ID │ Host   │ Scope │ CPU │ RAM │ Disk │ Status │
╞════╪════════╪═══════╪═════╪═════╪══════╪════════╡
│ 1  │ beaver │ dev   │ 4   │ 8G  │ 60G  │ up     │
│ 2  │ otter  │ build │ 8   │ 16G │ 120G │ up     │
│ 3  │ owl    │ test  │ 2   │ 4G  │ 40G  │ down   │
│ 4  │ hawk   │ infra │ 4   │ 8G  │ 80G  │ up     │
└────┴────────┴───────┴─────┴─────┴──────┴────────┘

▌ 4. Mixed width — one long column, rest short

┌────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┬─────┐
│ Host   │ Notes                                                         │ Up  │
╞════════╪═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╪═════╡
│ beaver │ Primary development box, runs the language servers and the    │ yes │
│        │ bulk of the day-to-day editing workload, so it gets the most  │     │
│        │ memory.                                                       │     │
├────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼─────┤
│ otter  │ Build and CI runner, kept deliberately clean so that build    │ yes │
│        │ failures are reproducible without local state leaking into    │     │
│        │ them.                                                         │     │
├────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼─────┤
│ owl    │ Currently down for maintenance.                               │ no  │
└────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┴─────┘

▌ 5. Table inside a list — known edge case

Third-party wrappers explicitly do not support this. Worth checking whether the
native one does.

• First item, with a table nested underneath it:

  ┌────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │ Key                │ Value                                                 │
  ╞════════════════════╪═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════╡
  │ Some fairly long   │ An even longer value that should need wrapping when   │
  │ key name here      │ the window is narrow enough to force it               │
  └────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

• Second item, plain text.

▌ 6. Long code block — should NOT wrap

                                                                           bash
 for vm in beaver otter owl hawk; do ssh "$vm" "PATH=\\$HOME/.local/bin:\\$PATH h
↪erdr update && echo done on $vm"; done


▌ 7. Long list items

• A list item long enough to need wrapping, which should break at a word
  boundary and stay visually indented under the bullet rather than returning to
  column zero on the continuation line.
• Short one.
  ◦ Nested item that is also quite long, to check that continuation lines
    respect the deeper indent level and do not collapse back to the parent
    bullet's indentation.

▌ What to check

1. Prose wraps at word boundaries (section 1)
2. Table cells wrap inside their columns, borders stay aligned (sections 2 and
   4)
3. Narrow the window — tables should re-fit rather than scroll
4. Cursor j/k through a wrapped table row moves one row, not one screen line
5. Code block does not wrap (section 6)
6. Nested table in section 5 renders at all"
`;

exports[`snapshots of the layout IR as text wrap-probe.md @ 80 1`] = `
"▌ Wrap probe

Table A — ordinary prose, no long tokens. Should wrap and fit.

┌────────┬────────────────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────┐
│ Option │ What it does                       │ When it bites                  │
╞════════╪════════════════════════════════════╪════════════════════════════════╡
│ first  │ Fits wide tables to the current    │ Only from v8.14.0 onward, so   │
│        │ window by wrapping individual      │ an older pin silently keeps    │
│        │ cells into several visual lines.   │ the old behaviour.             │
├────────┼────────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────┤
│ second │ Controls whether the line under    │ Disabling it means you never   │
│        │ the cursor stays rendered or       │ see raw pipes while editing a  │
│        │ reverts to raw source text.        │ row of the table.              │
└────────┴────────────────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────┘

Table B — one very long unbreakable token. Suspected overflow case.

┌────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────┐
│ Option │ Value                                     │ Note                    │
╞════════╪═══════════════════════════════════════════╪═════════════════════════╡
│ path   │ /Users/remzi/Projects/dotfiles/shared/    │ A single token that     │
│        │ config/nvim/lua/plugins/render-markdown.  │ cannot be broken at any │
│        │ lua                                       │ word boundary at all.   │
├────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────┤
│ url    │ https://github.com/MeanderingProgrammer/  │ Same again, even        │
│        │ render-markdown.nvim/blob/main/lua/       │ longer, also            │
│        │ render-markdown/parser/table.lua          │ unbreakable.            │
└────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────┘"
`;

